window.LESSONS = {"title":"Kullilli lessons","credit":"Based on the Kullilli Sketch Grammar (first edition, 2023) by Juhyae Kim, Irene Yi, Sam Lopez, Alida Scheffers, Jeremiah Jewell, Coralie Cram, Claire Bowern and Toby Adams.","units":[{"n":1,"title":"Getting started","intro":"<p>What this course is based on, and how to read the examples.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"name-kullilli","n":"1.1","title":"The name Kullilli","grammar":"Introduction › Spelling of the name Kullilli, p. 4","html":"<p>Books about Australian languages spell the name of the language in several ways, for example <em>Garlali</em> and <em>Kalali</em>. Austlang, the list of First languages of Australia kept by AIATSIS (austlang.aiatsis.gov.au), gives a few more spellings and more information about sources.</p><p>These lessons spell it <strong>Kullilli</strong>. This is the spelling the community prefers, it helps English speakers say the name the right way, and it is widely known.</p>","qa":[["Name two other spellings of Kullilli that you might find in older books.","<em>Garlali</em> and <em>Kalali</em>."],["Give one reason these lessons use the spelling Kullilli.","It is the community's preferred spelling. (It also helps English speakers say the name correctly, and it is widely known.)"],["Where can you find more spellings of the name and more about Kullilli sources?","In Austlang, the AIATSIS list of First languages of Australia."]]},{"id":"sources","n":"1.2","title":"Where our Kullilli comes from","grammar":"Introduction › About Kullilli sources, p. 4–5","html":"<p>What we know about Kullilli comes from records made with Kullilli speakers. There are six main sources:</p><ul><li>Gavan Breen's interviews with Paddy Ardoch and George Wallace, in the 1960s.</li><li>Nils Holmer's interviews with Paddy Ardoch, in the 1950s.</li><li>Stephen Wurm and Maryalice McDonald's interviews with Charlie Phillips.</li><li>Interviews with Charlie Phillips and Janet Mathews.</li><li>Luise Hercus and Claire Bowern's interviews with Peter Hood, in 1999.</li><li>Smaller word lists, such as those in <em>Science of Man</em> and in Curr's <em>The Australian Race</em> (1886).</li></ul><p>Some sources have a lot of information; others are mostly lists of words. These lessons use all of them, but they mostly follow the Kullilli of <strong>Charlie Phillips</strong>, as recorded by McDonald and Wurm, because that is the largest source and has the most sentences.</p>","qa":[["How many main sources of Kullilli are there?","Six."],["Whose Kullilli do these lessons mostly follow, and why?","Charlie Phillips's, as recorded by McDonald and Wurm, because it is the largest source with the most sentences."],["Which speaker did Luise Hercus and Claire Bowern work with in 1999?","Peter Hood."]]},{"id":"gaps-and-variation","n":"1.3","title":"What the records leave out","grammar":"Introduction › About Kullilli sources, p. 5","html":"<p>None of the sources has a very large vocabulary. They concentrate on plants, animals, basic actions and everyday words. Very few words for feelings or abstract ideas were written down. That is not because Kullilli people didn't talk about these things: the records are just not a complete record of the language.</p><p>Other things we talk about today didn't exist (or weren't common) in the 1950s and 1960s, such as mobile phones. There are no Kullilli words for them yet, but new words can be made. All languages make new words when new things come into use, and Kullilli is no different.</p><p>People also spoke Kullilli in different ways, just as people in America, England and Australia speak English differently. Some differences were in pronunciation and some were in the choice of words. The dictionary has more about which words different speakers used.</p>","qa":[["Why are there so few Kullilli words for feelings in the records?","Not because people didn't talk about feelings. The records are not a complete record of the language."],["Can Kullilli have words for new things like mobile phones?","Yes. All languages make new words when new things come into use; Kullilli words for these still need to be made."],["True or false: everyone spoke Kullilli in exactly the same way.","False. There were differences in pronunciation and in choice of words, just as there are in English."]]},{"id":"reading-examples","n":"1.4","title":"Reading the examples","grammar":"Introduction › Reading Kullilli examples, p. 6","html":"<p>Each Kullilli example has three lines. The first line is the Kullilli. The second line gives the English for each Kullilli word, right underneath it. The third line is a translation into everyday English.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidya</span><span class=\"g\">father</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go.back-is</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span><span class=\"g\">tomorrow</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My father is coming back tomorrow.’</figcaption></figure><p>So <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> means ‘my’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidya</span> means ‘father’, and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span> means ‘tomorrow’. A dash separates a word from its endings: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika-gala</span> is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika</span> ‘go back’ with the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span>. When one Kullilli part needs several English words, they are joined with dots (<em>go.back</em>).</p><p>Under some examples you will see a number like <em>Recorded sentence 146/18</em>: this is where the sentence comes from in the collection of recorded sentences. Examples marked <em>Made up by the guide's authors</em> were built from what we know about how Kullilli works; they were not recorded from a speaker.</p>","qa":[["What are the three lines of an example?","The Kullilli; the English for each Kullilli word; and a translation into everyday English."],["In the example, which word means ‘father’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidya</span>."],["What does it mean when an example is marked \"made up\"?","The guide's authors built it from what we know about Kullilli. It was not recorded from a speaker."]]},{"id":"word-parts","n":"1.5","title":"How Kullilli words work","grammar":"Types of Kullilli words, p. 17","html":"<p>In English, most words mean something on their own. In Kullilli, a word is often made of several parts, and each part adds some meaning.</p><p>So Kullilli often uses one word where English uses more than one. English says <em>from Brisbane</em>, but Kullilli says <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">Brisbane-andru</span>: the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> means ‘from’.</p><p>The order of words is different too. Kullilli verbs tend to come at the end of the sentence (though they don't have to), while English verbs go in the middle.</p><p>These lessons start with greetings and numbers, so you can start talking straight away, and then go on to verbs, nouns, pronouns, and the other kinds of words.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘from Brisbane’ in Kullilli?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">Brisbane-andru</span>."],["Which part of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">Brisbane-andru</span> means ‘from’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span>."],["Where do Kullilli verbs tend to go in a sentence?","At the end, though they don't have to."]]}]},{"n":2,"title":"Sounds and spelling","intro":"<p>How to read and say Kullilli words. Kullilli is written with English letters, but some letters (and pairs of letters) stand for different sounds, and some Kullilli sounds don't exist in English. You can listen to recordings of words in the dictionary while you practise. If you'd rather start talking straight away, you can skip ahead to Greetings and come back here later.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"vowels","n":"2.1","title":"Three vowels: a, i, u","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Vowels, p. 7","html":"<p>English has lots of vowel sounds, but Kullilli has only three: <strong>a</strong>, <strong>i</strong> and <strong>u</strong>.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>How to say it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>a</td><td>like the <em>u</em> in <em>but</em> (not the <em>a</em> in <em>apple</em>)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala</span> ‘hey’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">waka</span> ‘tree’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yara</span> ‘message’</td></tr><tr><td>i</td><td>like the <em>i</em> in <em>bit</em> (not the <em>i</em> in <em>I'm</em> or <em>idle</em>)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">girri</span> ‘stomach’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mirri</span> ‘dog’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyi</span> ‘fire’</td></tr><tr><td>u</td><td>like the <em>u</em> in <em>put</em> or the <em>oo</em> in <em>look</em> (not the <em>u</em> in <em>umbrella</em> or <em>cup</em>)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunu</span> ‘riverbank’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guturu</span> ‘swan’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">muthu</span> ‘boy, kids’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["How many vowel sounds does Kullilli have, and what are they?","Three: a, i and u."],["Is the <em>a</em> in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala</span> ‘hey’ said like the <em>a</em> in <em>apple</em>?","No. It is like the <em>u</em> in <em>but</em>."],["Which English word has a vowel like Kullilli <em>u</em>: <em>put</em> or <em>cup</em>?","<em>put</em>."]]},{"id":"easy-consonants","n":"2.2","title":"The easiest consonants: b, p, g, k, m, w, y","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › “Easiest” consonants, p. 8","html":"<p>Seven Kullilli consonant letters sound the same as, or very like, the same letters in English.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>How to say it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>b</td><td>as in <em>baby</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bala</span> ‘today’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">babadya</span> ‘husband’</td></tr><tr><td>p</td><td>as in <em>spy</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">kapi</span> ‘egg’</td></tr><tr><td>g</td><td>as in <em>gag</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galga</span> ‘evening’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gula</span> ‘grass’</td></tr><tr><td>k</td><td>as in <em>sky</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">kunduru</span> ‘black bream (fish)’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka</span> ‘strike’</td></tr><tr><td>m</td><td>as in <em>moon</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mala</span> ‘hand’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gumi</span> ‘big’</td></tr><tr><td>w</td><td>as in <em>wow</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">waliba</span> ‘help’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawa</span> ‘come’</td></tr><tr><td>y</td><td>as in <em>yes</em> (never as in <em>why</em> or <em>happy</em>)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yulu</span> ‘truthfully’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanggu</span> ‘spear’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["Which seven Kullilli consonants sound (almost) the same as in English?","b, p, g, k, m, w and y."],["Is Kullilli <em>y</em> ever a vowel, as in English <em>happy</em>?","No. It is always like the <em>y</em> in <em>yes</em>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawa</span> mean?","‘come’."]]},{"id":"ng","n":"2.3","title":"One sound, two letters: ng","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › “Mostly easy” consonants, p. 8–9","html":"<p><strong>ng</strong> is a single sound, like the <em>ng</em> in <em>sing</em>. It feels a bit like <em>n</em>, but with your tongue bunched up near the back of your mouth.</p><p>Unlike English, Kullilli <strong>ng</strong> can come at the beginning of a word.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngapa</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘water’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘ears’</span></li></ul>","qa":[["How many sounds does <em>ng</em> stand for?","One."],["How is Kullilli <em>ng</em> different from English <em>ng</em>?","It can come at the beginning of a word, as in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngapa</span> ‘water’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngapa</span> mean?","‘water’."]]},{"id":"r-and-rr","n":"2.4","title":"Two r sounds: r and rr","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › “Mostly easy” consonants, p. 8–9; The consonant sound rr, p. 12","html":"<p>Kullilli has more than one r-like sound.</p><p>A single <strong>r</strong> can be said in two ways, and both are sounds of Australian English: like the <em>r</em> in <em>rat</em>, or like the quick <em>tt</em> in <em>butter</em> (said fast, with an Australian accent). You may just need to learn for each word which way its <em>r</em> is said.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘who’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘think’</span></li></ul><p>A double <strong>rr</strong> is a different sound: a rolled or trilled <em>r</em>, like the <em>r</em> in Scottish English, Italian or Spanish.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘feather’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">murra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘camp’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garri</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘fish’</span></li></ul>","qa":[["What are the two ways a single <em>r</em> can be said?","Like the <em>r</em> in <em>rat</em>, or like the quick <em>tt</em> in <em>butter</em>."],["How is <em>rr</em> said?","As a rolled (trilled) <em>r</em>, as in Scottish English, Italian or Spanish."],["Are <em>r</em> and <em>rr</em> the same sound?","No, they are different sounds."]]},{"id":"d-t-n-l","n":"2.5","title":"d, t, n, l, and the start of a word","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › “Mostly easy” consonants, p. 8–9","html":"<p>Inside a word, <strong>d</strong>, <strong>t</strong>, <strong>n</strong> and <strong>l</strong> sound much as they do in Australian English (as in <em>adore</em>, <em>atop</em>, <em>another</em>, <em>alone</em>).</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manda</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘hold’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">miti</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘dirty’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘you’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘hand’</span></li></ul><p>But when a word <strong>begins</strong> with <em>d</em>, <em>t</em>, <em>n</em> or <em>l</em>, the sound changes: it is said with the tip of the tongue curled back, as if the word were spelled with <em>rd</em>, <em>rt</em>, <em>rn</em> or <em>rl</em> (see the lesson on curled-back sounds). For example, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">duna</span> ‘time’ is said as if it were spelled <em>rduna</em>.</p><p>This doesn't apply to words beginning with <em>dy, dh, ty, th, ny, nh, ly</em> or <em>lh</em>: those are sounds of their own (see the next lessons).</p>","qa":[["How is <em>d</em> said at the start of a word?","Like <em>rd</em>, with the tip of the tongue curled back."],["How is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">duna</span> ‘time’ pronounced?","As if it were spelled <em>rduna</em>."],["Does the rule apply to a word beginning with <em>dh</em>?","No. <em>dh</em> (like <em>dy, th, ty, nh, ny, lh, ly</em>) keeps its own sound."]]},{"id":"dental-sounds","n":"2.6","title":"Tongue between the teeth: dh, th, nh, lh","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Dental consonants, p. 10","html":"<p><strong>dh</strong>, <strong>th</strong>, <strong>nh</strong> and <strong>lh</strong> are each one sound, even though they are written with two letters (like <em>ch</em> in English). They are like <em>d</em>, <em>t</em>, <em>n</em> and <em>l</em>, but with the tip of your tongue <strong>between your front teeth</strong>. Each has an <em>h</em>: think of the <em>h</em> as a reminder to put your tongue between your teeth.</p><p>Kullilli <strong>th</strong> is not English <em>th</em>. The air stops completely before you let the sound go, so it sounds closer to English <em>t</em> than to English <em>th</em>.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>How to say it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>dh</td><td>like <em>d</em>, tongue between the teeth</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhuna</span> ‘time’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> ‘yesterday’</td></tr><tr><td>th</td><td>like <em>t</em>, tongue between the teeth</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi</span> ‘run’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi</span> ‘meat’</td></tr><tr><td>nh</td><td>like <em>n</em>, tongue between the teeth</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhadya</span> ‘see’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdhu</span> ‘often’</td></tr><tr><td>lh</td><td>like <em>l</em>, tongue between the teeth</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha</span> ‘sit’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walhtha</span> ‘take’</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Saying these sounds may be easier than hearing the difference between them and <em>d, t, n, l</em>. Listening to recordings in the dictionary helps.</p>","qa":[["Where does your tongue go for <em>dh</em>, <em>th</em>, <em>nh</em> and <em>lh</em>?","The tip goes between your front teeth."],["Is Kullilli <em>th</em> like the <em>th</em> in English <em>thin</em>?","No. The air stops completely, so it sounds closer to English <em>t</em>."],["What does the <em>h</em> in these letters remind you to do?","Put the tip of your tongue between your teeth."]]},{"id":"palatal-sounds","n":"2.7","title":"Middle of the tongue: dy, ty, ny, ly","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Palatal consonants, p. 10–11","html":"<p><strong>dy</strong>, <strong>ty</strong>, <strong>ny</strong> and <strong>ly</strong> are each one sound too. You say them with the <strong>middle</strong> of your tongue (not the tip) pressed against the roof of your mouth.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>How to say it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>dy</td><td>like <em>j</em> in <em>jar</em> or <em>d</em> in <em>dew</em>, but softer</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dyilpa</span> ‘dew’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganydya</span> ‘want’</td></tr><tr><td>ty</td><td>like <em>ch</em> in <em>choose</em>, <em>t</em> in <em>tune</em>, or <em>tch</em> in <em>catch</em>, but softer, with less air</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> ‘my’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yibi buntyu</span> ‘eyebrows’</td></tr><tr><td>ny</td><td>like <em>ny</em> in <em>canyon</em>, <em>gn</em> in <em>lasagna</em>, or <em>n</em> in <em>onion</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bindrinya</span> ‘grasshopper’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunytyu</span> ‘hair, fur’</td></tr><tr><td>ly</td><td>like <em>ll</em> in <em>million</em></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span> ‘far’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> ‘no’</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Remember that <em>y</em> is not a vowel in Kullilli. So <em>dy</em> is never said as in <em>ready</em>, <em>ty</em> never as in <em>beauty</em>, <em>ny</em> never as in <em>many</em>, and <em>ly</em> never as in <em>rally</em>.</p>","qa":[["Which part of the tongue touches the roof of the mouth for <em>dy</em>, <em>ty</em>, <em>ny</em> and <em>ly</em>?","The middle of the tongue (not the tip)."],["Is the <em>ny</em> in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bindrinya</span> ‘grasshopper’ said like the <em>ny</em> in <em>many</em>?","No. It is like the <em>ny</em> in <em>canyon</em>."],["Which English word has a sound like Kullilli <em>ly</em>?","<em>million</em> (the <em>ll</em>)."]]},{"id":"retroflex-sounds","n":"2.8","title":"Tongue curled back: rd, rt, rn, rl","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Retroflex consonants, p. 11–12","html":"<p>For <strong>rd</strong>, <strong>rt</strong>, <strong>rn</strong> and <strong>rl</strong>, curl the tip of your tongue up and back until the underside of your tongue just touches the roof of your mouth. Then say <em>d</em>, <em>t</em>, <em>n</em> or <em>l</em>. The tongue position is a bit like the one for the <em>r</em> in English <em>rat</em>.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>How to say it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>rd</td><td>like <em>d</em>, tongue tip curled back</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burdu</span> ‘windbreak’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gardra</span> ‘spear’</td></tr><tr><td>rt</td><td>like <em>t</em>, tongue tip curled back</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">birta</span> ‘night’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warta</span> ‘top of leg, thigh’</td></tr><tr><td>rn</td><td>like <em>n</em>, tongue tip curled back</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warnpa</span> ‘mud’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barna</span> ‘goanna’</td></tr><tr><td>rl</td><td>like <em>l</em>, tongue tip curled back</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">durlku</span> ‘heart’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurlu</span> ‘knee’</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You can probably feel the difference from <em>d, t, n, l</em> more easily than you can hear it. And remember: a word that <strong>starts</strong> with <em>d, t, n</em> or <em>l</em> is said with these curled-back sounds.</p>","qa":[["What do you do with your tongue for <em>rd</em>, <em>rt</em>, <em>rn</em> and <em>rl</em>?","Curl the tip up and back so the underside touches the roof of your mouth."],["Which curled-back sound is in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barna</span> ‘goanna’?","<em>rn</em>."],["Which words are said with a curled-back sound even though they're not spelled with <em>r</em>?","Words that start with <em>d</em>, <em>t</em>, <em>n</em> or <em>l</em>."]]},{"id":"sound-chart","n":"2.9","title":"All the sounds at a glance","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Summary of Kullilli sounds, p. 12–13","html":"<p>The vowels are simple: <strong>a</strong>, <strong>i</strong>, <strong>u</strong>. This chart shows all the consonants. The top row says where in the mouth the sound is made; the left column says how it is made.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>lips</th><th>teeth</th><th>gum ridge</th><th>roof of mouth</th><th>tongue curled back</th><th>back of mouth</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>stops (voiced)</td><td>b</td><td>dh</td><td>d</td><td>dy</td><td>rd</td><td>g</td></tr><tr><td>stops (voiceless)</td><td>p</td><td>th</td><td>t</td><td>ty</td><td>rt</td><td>k</td></tr><tr><td>nasals</td><td>m</td><td>nh</td><td>n</td><td>ny</td><td>rn</td><td>ng</td></tr><tr><td>l-sounds</td><td></td><td>lh</td><td>l</td><td>ly</td><td>rl</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>r-sounds</td><td></td><td></td><td>r, rr</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>glides</td><td>w</td><td></td><td></td><td>y</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Linguists call these places <em>bilabial</em> (lips), <em>dental</em> (teeth), <em>alveolar</em> (gum ridge), <em>palatal</em> (roof of mouth), <em>retroflex</em> (curled back) and <em>velar</em> (back of mouth).</p><aside class=\"note\"><p>Remember: <em>d, t, n, l</em> at the start of a word are said like <em>rd, rt, rn, rl</em>; and <em>r</em> can be said like the <em>r</em> in <em>rat</em> or the <em>tt</em> in <em>butter</em>.</p></aside>","qa":[["Which column do <em>dh</em>, <em>th</em>, <em>nh</em> and <em>lh</em> belong to?","The teeth (dental) column."],["Which sounds are made at the back of the mouth?","<em>g</em>, <em>k</em> and <em>ng</em>."],["Which nasal sound goes with <em>dy</em> and <em>ty</em>?","<em>ny</em>."]]},{"id":"dash","n":"2.10","title":"The dash: roots and endings","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Using the dash ‘-’, p. 13–14","html":"<p>The dash (<strong>-</strong>) is not a letter. It separates a root word from its endings, a bit like the dash in English <em>non-traditional</em> or <em>semi-circle</em>, but Kullilli uses it much more: in these lessons, every ending is separated from the word it goes on.</p><p>For example, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marpa</span> means ‘cook’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> is the future ending. Together they make <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marpa-rra</span> ‘will cook’.</p><p>As your Kullilli improves, you may not need the dashes any more. Write them or leave them out, whichever you prefer: they make the parts of words easier to see.</p>","qa":[["Is the dash a letter of the Kullilli alphabet?","No. It separates a root word from its endings."],["Put <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marpa</span> ‘cook’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> (future) together. What does it mean?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marpa-rra</span> ‘will cook’."],["Do you have to write the dashes?","No, it's up to you."]]},{"id":"stress","n":"2.11","title":"Stress: always the first syllable","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Stress, p. 14","html":"<p><em>Stress</em> is the syllable said with the most emphasis. In English it can fall anywhere: TELevision, anniVERsary, underSTAND.</p><p>Kullilli is much easier: stress is <strong>always on the first syllable</strong>.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘my’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘yesterday’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barna</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘goanna’</span></li></ul><p>Go back over the words in the earlier lessons and practise putting the emphasis on the first part of each word.</p>","qa":[["Which syllable is stressed in Kullilli words?","The first."],["Where is the stress in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span> ‘tomorrow’?","On the first syllable, <em>wi</em>."],["Is Kullilli stress like English stress?","No. English stress can fall on different syllables; Kullilli stress is always on the first."]]},{"id":"old-spellings","n":"2.12","title":"Why old sources spell words differently","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Spelling in different Kullilli sources, p. 14–15","html":"<p>You don't need to learn other spellings to speak Kullilli, but if you read the older sources you will meet them.</p><ul><li>The missionaries and older sources (before about the 1940s) spelled words as if they were English. They weren't consistent, they missed details that matter in Aboriginal languages, and what they wrote can often be read in several ways.</li><li>Some later sources are very precise about every detail of the sounds, with many special symbols. This helps us work out what the older sources meant.</li><li>Other sources write only the differences in sound that change meaning.</li></ul><p>Holmer's (1988) description of Paddy Ardoch's Kullilli is mostly consistent. Breen is fairly regular and adds extra detail. Tindale adds a lot of detail but usually isn't very accurate. McDonald and Wurm's record of Charlie Phillips is the most consistent, and the largest: over 500 sentences.</p>","qa":[["How did the older sources (before the 1940s) spell Kullilli words?","As if they were English, inconsistently and without important details."],["Which source is the most consistent and the largest?","McDonald and Wurm's record of Charlie Phillips (over 500 sentences)."],["Do you need to learn the old spellings to speak Kullilli?","No, but they are useful if you read the older sources."]]},{"id":"spelling-table","n":"2.13","title":"Old letters and modern letters","grammar":"The Kullilli Alphabet &amp; Sounds › Spelling in different Kullilli sources, p. 15–16","html":"<p>This table shows how some of the researchers wrote the sounds that these lessons spell with two letters. A question mark means we aren't sure what the researcher meant.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Modern Kullilli</th><th>McDonald &amp; Wurm</th><th>Breen</th><th>Sharpe</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>dy</td><td>ᶁ</td><td>dy</td><td>j (also dy?)</td></tr><tr><td>ty</td><td>ƫ</td><td>ty</td><td>č</td></tr><tr><td>ny</td><td>ɲ</td><td>ny</td><td>ñ</td></tr><tr><td>ly</td><td>ᶅ</td><td>ly</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>dh</td><td>d̯</td><td>d̯ (also ð?)</td><td>ð (also d?)</td></tr><tr><td>th</td><td>t̯</td><td>t̯</td><td>θ</td></tr><tr><td>nh</td><td>n̯</td><td>n̯</td><td>n̯</td></tr><tr><td>lh</td><td>l̯</td><td>l̯</td><td>l̯?</td></tr><tr><td>rd</td><td>ḍ</td><td>ḍ</td><td>d? ḍ?</td></tr><tr><td>rt</td><td>ṭ</td><td>ṭ</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>rn</td><td>ṇ</td><td>ṇ</td><td>n?</td></tr><tr><td>rl</td><td>ḷ</td><td>ḷ</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>ng</td><td>ŋ</td><td>ŋ</td><td>ŋ</td></tr><tr><td>r</td><td>ṛ</td><td>ṛ</td><td>r̆? r̃?</td></tr><tr><td>rr</td><td>r</td><td>r̃</td><td>r̆? r̃?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The dictionary often lists several spellings of a word, so that people can find the word they are looking for. For the authors of the sketch grammar, it matters far more that people use Kullilli and get comfortable with it than that there is one way of spelling each word.</p>","qa":[["How did McDonald and Wurm write the sound these lessons spell <em>ng</em>?","As ŋ."],["Why does the dictionary list several spellings for some words?","So that people can find the word they are looking for, whichever spelling they know."],["Which matters more to the authors: one standard spelling, or people using the language?","People using the language."]]}]},{"n":3,"title":"Greetings and introductions","intro":"<p>Words and sentences for greeting people and introducing yourself. Some of these phrases were recorded from Kullilli speakers; others were made up by the guide's authors, based on how Kullilli and other Aboriginal languages work. Each one is marked.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"hello","n":"3.1","title":"Saying hello","grammar":"Greetings &amp; Introductions › Greetings, p. 18","html":"<p>The sources have few examples of greetings. Across Aboriginal languages there are generally two ways to greet people. One is to translate English greetings such as ‘good morning’. The other is found in languages where older greetings were recorded: phrases like ‘what news?’ or ‘where are you going?’, such as Bardi <em>anggi jawal</em> ‘what news’ and Yolŋu <em>nhämirr</em> ‘what-having’.</p><p>Something similar in Kullilli could be <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina yara-ani</span>, literally ‘what message’, a bit like English <em>what's up?</em>. This phrase is not recorded: the guide's authors made it up.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina</span><span class=\"g\">what</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yara-ani</span><span class=\"g\">message</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘What news? (like ‘What's up?’)’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure><p>Another way to greet someone is to ask where they are going. This sentence was recorded from Charlie Phillips:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-anga</span><span class=\"g\">where-to</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where are you going?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 181/53</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina yara-ani</span> mean, word for word?","‘what message’."],["Was <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina yara-ani</span> recorded from a Kullilli speaker?","No. The guide's authors made it up, based on greetings in other Aboriginal languages."],["How do you ask ‘Where are you going?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-anga ini yanhtha-gala</span>."]]},{"id":"goodbye","n":"3.2","title":"Saying goodbye","grammar":"Greetings &amp; Introductions › Greetings, p. 18–19","html":"<p>To say goodbye, you could say ‘I'll see you soon’. This sentence was made up by the guide's authors:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guntu</span><span class=\"g\">soon</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-rra</span><span class=\"g\">see-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I'll see you soon.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure><p>If you know when you'll see them, change <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guntu</span> ‘soon’ to <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span> ‘tomorrow’ or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarlawatyu</span> ‘the day after tomorrow’.</p><p>This one is a real example from Charlie Phillips:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba-rra</span><span class=\"g\">go-away-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I'm going away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 180/52</p></figure><p>For ‘good night’ you could say <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali ngawu</span>, made from <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali</span> ‘good’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngawu</span> ‘night’. This isn't recorded as a phrase; the authors put the two words together.</p>","qa":[["How would you say ‘I'll see you tomorrow’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla ngathu nadya-rra inha</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi yanhtha-ba-rra</span> mean?","‘I'm going away.’"],["Which two words make up <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali ngawu</span> ‘good night’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali</span> ‘good’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngawu</span> ‘night’."]]},{"id":"hey","n":"3.3","title":"Hey! gala and gari","grammar":"Greetings &amp; Introductions › Greetings, p. 19","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gari</span> mean something like ‘hey’, and might be used to catch someone's attention. (In some languages, like Bardi, a word like this is used more to wrap up a conversation or a story.)</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala</span><span class=\"g\">hey</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span><span class=\"g\">you.all</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Hey, you all.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gari</span><span class=\"g\">hey</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha</span><span class=\"g\">go</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">/</span><span class=\"g\">/</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-rra</span><span class=\"g\">see-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Hey, don't go away, I want to see you.’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["Give two Kullilli words for ‘hey’.","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gari</span>."],["How do you say ‘Hey, you all’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala yurra</span>."],["In the second example, which word means ‘not’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span>."]]},{"id":"names","n":"3.4","title":"What's your name?","grammar":"Greetings &amp; Introductions › Introductions, p. 19","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarinya</span> means ‘name’. To ask someone their name:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wani</span><span class=\"g\">what</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">your</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarinya-ani</span><span class=\"g\">name</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘What's your name?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 332/12</p></figure><p>To answer, or to introduce yourself first, put your name in the gap, with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> after it:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarinya-ani</span><span class=\"g\">name-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">____-nha</span><span class=\"g\">____</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My name is ____.’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["What is the Kullilli word for ‘name’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarinya</span>."],["How do you ask someone their name?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wani ingka yarinya-ani</span>."],["How would someone called Toby say ‘My name is Toby’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya yarinya-ani Toby-nha</span>."]]},{"id":"where-from","n":"3.5","title":"Where are you from?","grammar":"Greetings &amp; Introductions › Introductions, p. 19–20","html":"<p>When you meet someone, you might ask where they are from:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru</span><span class=\"g\">where-from</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where are you from?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 133/5</p></figure><p>Kullilli has several words for ‘you’: one for one person (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span>), one for two people (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span>), and one for three or more (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span>). (There's more about this in the Pronouns unit.)</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru</span><span class=\"g\">where-from</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span><span class=\"g\">you.two</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where are you two from?’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru</span><span class=\"g\">where-from</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span><span class=\"g\">you.all</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where are you all from?’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["Which word for ‘you’ do you use when talking to two people?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span>."],["How do you ask a group of five people where they are from?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru yurra</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> mean in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru</span>?","‘from’."]]},{"id":"im-from","n":"3.6","title":"I'm from…","grammar":"Greetings &amp; Introductions › Introductions, p. 20","html":"<p>To say where you are from, put <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> ‘from’ on the place:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-nuga-andru</span><span class=\"g\">water-big-from</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘(I'm, we're) from the big water.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 134/6</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">____-andru</span><span class=\"g\">____-from</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘(I'm, we're) from ____.’</figcaption></figure><p>You can use any place name, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">Brisbane-andru</span> ‘from Brisbane’ or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">Dynevor-andru</span> ‘from Dynevor’.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘I'm from Brisbane’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">Brisbane-andru</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-nuga-andru</span> mean?","‘from the big water’."],["Where does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> go: before or after the place name?","After it, joined on as an ending."]]},{"id":"speak-kullilli","n":"3.7","title":"Do you speak Kullilli?","grammar":"Greetings &amp; Introductions › Introductions, p. 20","html":"<p>One more question to practise with other learners, with two answers to choose from:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-gala</span><span class=\"g\">speak-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garlala-nha</span><span class=\"g\">Kullilli</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Do you speak Kullilli?’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ga</span><span class=\"g\">yes</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">waywa</span><span class=\"g\">small</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-gala</span><span class=\"g\">speak-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Yes, I speak a little.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali</span><span class=\"g\">good</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-gala</span><span class=\"g\">speak-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I speak well.’</figcaption></figure><p>Now you can greet people, and ask and answer three questions when you meet someone for the first time.</p>","qa":[["What is the Kullilli for ‘yes’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ga</span>."],["How do you say ‘I speak well’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali nganyi yanhdha-gala</span>."],["Which word in the question means ‘Kullilli’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garlala-nha</span>."]]}]},{"n":4,"title":"Numbers and amounts","intro":"<p>Counting from one to four, and words for ‘all’ and ‘many’.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"one-two","n":"4.1","title":"One and two","grammar":"Numbers › Numerals 1–4, p. 22","html":"<p>The Kullilli records we have only include the numbers one to four. We can't say for sure whether there were only words up to four, or whether there were more that nobody wrote down.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dharrany</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘one’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘one, a’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘two’</span></li></ul><p>There are two ways to say ‘one’. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dharrany</span> can be used on its own, but <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> must be attached to another word, as in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-watyu</span> ‘one man, a man’.</p>","qa":[["What is the Kullilli for ‘two’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu</span>."],["What are the two ways to say ‘one’, and how are they different?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dharrany</span>, which stands on its own, and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span>, which is attached to another word."],["Which numbers do the Kullilli records include?","One to four."]]},{"id":"watyu","n":"4.2","title":"-watyu: ‘one’ or ‘a’","grammar":"Numbers › Numerals 1–4, p. 22–23","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> is often translated as ‘one’, but also as ‘a’ or ‘an’. In English, <em>a man</em> means any one man, while <em>the man</em> means a particular man. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> sometimes has this ‘any one’ meaning, like English <em>a</em>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-watyu</span><span class=\"g\">man-one</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">waga-gala</span><span class=\"g\">lay-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walbiri-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">shade-locative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘A man (that one) is lying in the shade.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 146/18</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">grasp-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-watyu-anha</span><span class=\"g\">stick-one-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I'll pick up a stick, that one, for you.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 115/51</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-watyu</span> mean?","‘one man’ or ‘a man’."],["Can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> be used as a word on its own?","No, it has to be attached to another word."],["In 115/51, which word means ‘a stick’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-watyu-anha</span>."]]},{"id":"three-four","n":"4.3","title":"Three and four","grammar":"Numbers › Numerals 1–4, p. 22","html":"<p>Once you know <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dharrany</span> ‘one’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu</span> ‘two’, three and four are easy: Kullilli makes them from one and two.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu dharrany</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘three (literally ‘two one’)’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu barrkulu</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘four (literally ‘two two’)’</span></li></ul>","qa":[["How do you say ‘three’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu dharrany</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu barrkulu</span> mean, word for word, and what number is it?","‘two two’, which means four."],["Which two numbers are used to make three and four?","One (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dharrany</span>) and two (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu</span>)."]]},{"id":"number-systems","n":"4.4","title":"Number systems in Australian languages","grammar":"Numbers › A note on numbers, p. 23","html":"<p>Some languages have numbers you can keep combining as far as you like. Others have numbers up to five or ten, or sometimes none at all. Australia's Indigenous languages vary a lot: some had no numbers that we know of, some had a few (one, two and three, for example), and some had numbers you could count with without limit.</p><p>Think about how often you actually use a big number like <em>one thousand, three hundred and fifty-four</em>: probably not very often.</p><p>Some Aboriginal communities have made new numbers. Warlpiri, for example, now has a word for seven (from the word for a boomerang shaped like a 7); eight is a double seed pod; and nine is a cup (a cup with a handle, seen from above, looks a bit like a 9).</p>","qa":[["Did all Aboriginal languages have the same kind of number system?","No. Some had no numbers we know of, some had a few, and some could count without limit."],["How did Warlpiri make a word for seven?","From the word for a boomerang shaped like a 7."],["Can communities make new number words?","Yes. Warlpiri, for example, has new words for seven, eight and nine."]]},{"id":"counting","n":"4.5","title":"Counting things","grammar":"Numbers › Numerals 1–4, p. 22–23","html":"<p>To count things, the number usually comes <strong>before</strong> the noun:</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dharrany garna</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘one person’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu garna</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘two people’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu dharrany garna</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘three people’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu barrkulu garna</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘four people’</span></li></ul><p>To use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> for ‘one’ instead, say <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-watyu</span> ‘one person’.</p><p>English adds <em>-s</em> when there is more than one: one dog, two dogs. In Kullilli (as in many languages of the world) you can just use the number without changing anything else: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dharrany thithi</span> ‘one dog’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu thithi</span> ‘two dogs’ (literally ‘two dog’).</p>","qa":[["Where does the number usually go: before or after the noun?","Before the noun."],["How do you say ‘three people’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu dharrany garna</span>."],["Does the word for ‘dog’ change in ‘two dogs’?","No: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu thithi</span>, literally ‘two dog’."]]},{"id":"buka-wandru","n":"4.6","title":"All and many: buka and wandru","grammar":"Numbers › Non-numeric amounts, p. 23–24","html":"<p>English also talks about amounts without numbers, with words like <em>some</em>, <em>many</em> and <em>all</em>. Kullilli has:</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘all’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘much, many, a lot’</span></li></ul><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> ‘all’ doesn't take endings; it is always used on its own, most often after the noun. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> can be used on its own:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span><span class=\"g\">lot</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-dhika-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go-go.back-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘A mob is coming.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 82/18</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> can also take endings, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span>. Here <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> are used together:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru-ani</span><span class=\"g\">lot-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana</span><span class=\"g\">3pl</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-ani</span><span class=\"g\">man-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">girni-ani</span><span class=\"g\">strong-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span><span class=\"g\">all</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The mob of men (together) are strong.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 213/21</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> mean?","‘all’."],["Can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> take endings?","No, it is always used on its own."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> mean, and can it take endings?","‘much, many, a lot’. Yes, it can, as in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru-ani</span>."]]},{"id":"two-bula","n":"4.7","title":"Two of something: -bula","grammar":"Numbers › Non-numeric amounts, p. 24","html":"<p>When you talk about how many people or things there are, the number can come before or after the noun. When you talk about two things, the noun can also have the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-bula</span>, which means ‘two of them’ (linguists call this <em>dual</em>).</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu</span><span class=\"g\">two</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-bula</span><span class=\"g\">dog-dual</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Two dogs.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 79/15</p></figure><p>In this sentence ‘two’ comes after the pronoun <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span> ‘the two of us’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">1dl-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barkulu-lu</span><span class=\"g\">two-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gangga-rra</span><span class=\"g\">carry-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of us will carry this one.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 383/63</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-bula</span> add to a noun?","It shows there are two (dual)."],["How do you say ‘two dogs’ using <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-bula</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barrkulu dhithi-bula</span>."],["In 383/63, does ‘two’ come before or after the word for ‘the two of us’?","After."]]}]},{"n":5,"title":"Verbs and time","intro":"<p>Verbs are words for actions or states: in English, <em>run</em>, <em>is</em> and <em>talk</em> are verbs. English adds <em>-s</em>, <em>-ed</em> or <em>will</em> to a verb to show when something happens. Kullilli adds endings to the verb.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"present-gala","n":"5.1","title":"Now: -gala","grammar":"Verbs › Present: -gala, p. 25","html":"<p>To talk about what is happening now, add the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span> to the verb. This is called the <em>present tense</em>.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘thinking’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘coming’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘laughing’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dunda-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘growing’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marnnga-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘making’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngara-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘hearing’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘giving’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngulka-gala</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘learning’</span></li></ul>","qa":[["Which ending do you add to a verb to say something is happening now?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngara-gala</span> mean?","‘hearing’."],["<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marpa</span> means ‘cook’. How do you say ‘cooking’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marpa-gala</span>."]]},{"id":"present-sentences","n":"5.2","title":"Verbs can go anywhere","grammar":"Verbs › Present: -gala, p. 25","html":"<p>In English, the verb usually comes after the subject (the person or thing doing the action). Kullilli verbs can come at the beginning, in the middle, or at the end of a sentence. These sentences are from Charlie Phillips:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">laugh-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span><span class=\"g\">that</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garnaani</span><span class=\"g\">man</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That man is laughing.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawalyalu</span><span class=\"g\">uncle</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marnnga-gala</span><span class=\"g\">make-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakarranyi</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Uncle is making a boomerang.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 99/35</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yulanha</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-gala</span><span class=\"g\">give-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I give it to you.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 71/7</p></figure>","qa":[["In ‘That man is laughing’, where is the verb?","At the beginning: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gala</span>."],["Can a Kullilli verb come at the end of a sentence?","Yes. It can come at the beginning, in the middle, or at the end."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawalyalu marnnga-gala bakarranyi</span> mean?","‘Uncle is making a boomerang.’"]]},{"id":"past-endings","n":"5.3","title":"The past: two endings","grammar":"Verbs › Past, p. 26","html":"<p>For things that have already happened (the <em>past tense</em>), Kullilli has two endings: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span>.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-nga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘came’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bura-nga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘told’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burli-nga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘fell’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garki-nga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘called’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-nga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘ran’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngalka-nga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘thought’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngulka-nga</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘learned’</span></li></ul><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘came’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bura-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘told’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gatyi-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘broke’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘asked’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mama-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘stole’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mukari-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘forgot’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘looked’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yantha-gali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘went’</span></li></ul>","qa":[["What are the two past endings?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span>."],["Give two ways to say ‘came’.","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-nga</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-gali</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mama-gali</span> mean?","‘stole’."]]},{"id":"past-nga","n":"5.4","title":"Finished actions: -nga","grammar":"Verbs › Past 1: -nga, p. 26","html":"<p>The difference between the two past endings is subtle. Generally, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span> is used for <strong>completed</strong> actions. So <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-nga</span> means ‘ran’ or ‘have run, had run’. It can't mean an action that was still going on, like ‘was running’ or ‘had been running’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-nga</span><span class=\"g\">run-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span><span class=\"g\">that</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwani</span><span class=\"g\">child</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That child ran.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhanagurungu</span><span class=\"g\">all.of.them</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">muraba-nga</span><span class=\"g\">finish-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gungaanha</span><span class=\"g\">food</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘All of them have finished this food.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 1</p></figure>","qa":[["What kind of past action is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span> used for?","Completed (finished) actions."],["Can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-nga</span> mean ‘was running’?","No. It means ‘ran’ or ‘have/had run’."],["How do you say ‘That child ran’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-nga nhaniguru warliwani</span>."]]},{"id":"past-gali","n":"5.5","title":"Ongoing actions in the past: -gali","grammar":"Verbs › Past 2: -gali, p. 27","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span> can be used for actions that were <strong>going on</strong> in the past. So <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-gali</span> can mean ‘came’ or ‘was coming’, but not ‘have come’ or ‘had come’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mukarri-gali</span><span class=\"g\">forget-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nungka</span><span class=\"g\">his</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarrinya</span><span class=\"g\">name</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I forgot his name.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">laugh-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span><span class=\"g\">that</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garnaani</span><span class=\"g\">man</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That man was laughing.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gari</span><span class=\"g\">hey</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-gali</span><span class=\"g\">see-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha</span><span class=\"g\">sit</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yalairri</span><span class=\"g\">there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garnaani</span><span class=\"g\">man</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-dhika-gali</span><span class=\"g\">go-go.back-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Hey, when you were looking over there, did a man go past?’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["Can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-gali</span> mean ‘was coming’?","Yes. It can mean ‘came’ or ‘was coming’."],["How would you say ‘That man was laughing’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gali nhaniguru garnaani</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mukarri-gali nganyi nungka yarrinya</span> mean?","‘I forgot his name.’"]]},{"id":"nga-or-gali","n":"5.6","title":"-nga or -gali?","grammar":"Verbs › Difference between -nga and -gali, p. 27","html":"<p>Don't worry too much about which to use. For a simple past event, either ending should work, and often the meaning seems the same. The guide's authors think <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span> is used when there is a <strong>time word</strong> in the sentence, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> ‘yesterday’. So even when an action is finished (which should mean <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span>), you can use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span> if there's a time word.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-nga</span><span class=\"g\">come-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngalingala</span><span class=\"g\">to.the.two.of.us</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span><span class=\"g\">that</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garnaani</span><span class=\"g\">man</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That man came to the two of us.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidya</span><span class=\"g\">father</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">come-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span><span class=\"g\">yesterday</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My father came yesterday.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-nga</span><span class=\"g\">ask-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span><span class=\"g\">him</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I asked him.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gali</span><span class=\"g\">ask-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span><span class=\"g\">him</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span><span class=\"g\">yesterday</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I asked him yesterday.’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["Which past ending seems to be used when there is a time word like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> ‘yesterday’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span>."],["How do you say ‘I asked him yesterday’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gali ngathu nhinha niladha</span>."],["For a simple past event, does it matter much which ending you use?","Not much: either should work."]]},{"id":"future-rra","n":"5.7","title":"The future: -rra","grammar":"Verbs › Future: -rra, p. 28","html":"<p>To talk about the future, use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span>. It doesn't matter how near or far in the future: you can use it for anything that happens after you talk about it. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> means ‘will’ or ‘going to’: if <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaltha</span> means ‘eat’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaltha-rra</span> means ‘will eat’.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barnrdi-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will cross’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaltha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will eat’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dumpa-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will show’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will strike’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will sit’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulpa-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will light’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will run’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will get’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marpa-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will cook’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngartandi-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will jump’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngudya-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will give’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">waga-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will lay’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘will go’</span></li></ul>","qa":[["What does the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> mean?","‘will’ or ‘going to’ (future)."],["Does it matter how far in the future the event is?","No."],["How do you say ‘will run’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-rra</span>."]]},{"id":"future-sentences","n":"5.8","title":"The future in sentences","grammar":"Verbs › Future: -rra, p. 28","html":"<p>Again, the verb can come at the beginning, in the middle or at the end of the sentence.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">grasp-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-watyu-anha</span><span class=\"g\">stick-one-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I'll pick up a stick, that one, for you.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 115/51</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ga</span><span class=\"g\">yes</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">go-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingala</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Yes, I will go to you.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyinha</span><span class=\"g\">fire</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span><span class=\"g\">think</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngalingu</span><span class=\"g\">we</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulpa-rra</span><span class=\"g\">light-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I think we will light a fire.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 151/23</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ga nganyi yanhtha-rra ingala</span> mean?","‘Yes, I will go to you.’"],["In 151/23, which word is the verb with the future ending?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulpa-rra</span> ‘will light’."],["Where is the verb in 115/51?","At the beginning: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-rra</span>."]]},{"id":"mood-endings","n":"5.9","title":"More than time: other verb endings","grammar":"Verbs › Other verb endings, p. 29","html":"<p>Verbs can tell us more than <em>when</em> something happens. A speaker might want to say how likely or certain something is, or how they feel about it. English does this with separate words like <em>should</em>, <em>must</em> and <em>can</em>: <em>She should/must/can do her homework.</em></p><p>Kullilli does it with other verb endings. They go on the verb <strong>instead of</strong> a tense ending: you use either a tense ending like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span>, or one of these.</p><ul><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span>: might happen, or someone wishes it would (<em>irrealis</em>)</li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span>: will certainly happen, or is about to (<em>factive</em>)</li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span>: could happen, or someone is able to do it (<em>possibility</em>)</li></ul>","qa":[["Can a verb have both <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span>?","No. These endings go on the verb instead of the tense endings."],["How does English usually show meanings like ‘should’ or ‘can’?","With separate words such as <em>should</em>, <em>must</em> and <em>can</em>."],["Which ending shows that something will certainly happen?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span>."]]},{"id":"irrealis-rri","n":"5.10","title":"Might happen: -rri","grammar":"Verbs › Irrealis: -rri, p. 29–30","html":"<p><em>Irrealis</em> is a Latin word meaning ‘unreal’: things that won't happen, didn't happen, or aren't certain. Kullilli uses the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span> when talking about wishes, things we think might happen, and things we aren't sure about.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">you.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-rri</span><span class=\"g\">give-irrealis</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-nha</span><span class=\"g\">1dl-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You (might) give to the two of us.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 282/26</p></figure><p>The person listening will probably give something to the two speakers, but it isn't guaranteed.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-lha</span><span class=\"g\">give-factive</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakarranyi</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-dhika-rri</span><span class=\"g\">give-go.back-irrealis</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi</span><span class=\"g\">meat</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘When/if I give back the boomerang, you will give me back the meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 199/7</p></figure><p>Here the speaker would like the listener to give back the meat, but there's no guarantee the listener will agree.</p>","qa":[["What does the word <em>irrealis</em> mean?","‘Unreal’: things that are not (or not yet) real or certain."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span> on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-rri</span> tell us about the giving?","It might happen, but it isn't guaranteed."],["Which ending in 199/7 shows what the speaker wishes would happen?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span>, in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-dhika-rri</span>."]]},{"id":"factive-lha","n":"5.11","title":"Sure to happen: -lha","grammar":"Verbs › Factive: -lha, p. 30","html":"<p>The <em>factive</em> ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span> is the opposite of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span>: it shows the speaker is <strong>certain</strong> the event will happen. It is called <em>factive</em> because it's a fact that the thing will happen.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhuna-bari</span><span class=\"g\">time-think</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span><span class=\"g\">we.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wira-rra</span><span class=\"g\">wait-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burli-lha</span><span class=\"g\">fall-factive</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-ani</span><span class=\"g\">water-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of us will wait until the water falls.’</figcaption><p class=\"lit\">Literally: ‘The two of us will wait, the water will fall.’</p><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 178/50</p></figure><p>The speaker knows that the water will fall, so they use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span> on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burli</span> ‘fall’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span><span class=\"g\">tomorrow</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mirla</span><span class=\"g\">sun</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-lha</span><span class=\"g\">rise-factive</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Tomorrow when the sun comes up…’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Part of a longer sentence</p></figure><p>The speaker knows for a fact that the sun will come up tomorrow.</p>","qa":[["What does the factive ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span> show?","That the speaker is certain the event will happen."],["Why is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span> used on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burli</span> ‘fall’ in 178/50?","Because the speaker knows the water will fall."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla mirla warlpa-lha</span> mean?","‘Tomorrow when the sun comes up…’"]]},{"id":"factive-now","n":"5.12","title":"About to: -lha","grammar":"Verbs › Factive: -lha, p. 30","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span> has a second use: it can show that an action is happening right away, often translated as ‘now’. It's like English <em>about to</em>, as in <em>I'm about to run</em>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span><span class=\"g\">okay</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-lha</span><span class=\"g\">run-factive</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘OK, I run (right now).’</figcaption></figure><p>The first word, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span> ‘OK’, also shows that the speaker is sure about what they are saying, so it fits well with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span>.</p>","qa":[["What English phrase is like the second use of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span>?","‘about to’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span> mean?","‘OK’."],["Give the two uses of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span>.","Showing an event is certain to happen, and showing an action is about to happen right away."]]},{"id":"possibility-langu","n":"5.13","title":"Could happen: -langu","grammar":"Verbs › Possibility: -langu, p. 31","html":"<p>The ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span> is a lot like English <em>can</em> or <em>could</em>. It shows that something might or could happen, or that someone is able to do something. It is sometimes written <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span>; we aren't sure yet whether these are two different endings, or one ending written in two ways.</p><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span> is similar in meaning to <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span>, but <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span> is used more for asking for things to happen, while <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span> is more about someone being <strong>able</strong> to do something, or something being <strong>possible</strong>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dratya-langa</span><span class=\"g\">bite-possibility</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhandru-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.erg-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">waliwa-nha</span><span class=\"g\">child-acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘It could bite that kid.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 97/33</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gatyi-li-ba-langu</span><span class=\"g\">break-apart-motion-possibility</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘(It) might break.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 106/42</p></figure><p>Something is able to bite, or to break, but it doesn't mean it will happen.</p>","qa":[["Which English words is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span> like?","<em>can</em> and <em>could</em>."],["What is the difference between <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span> is used more for asking for things to happen; <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span> is more about ability or possibility."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gatyi-li-ba-langu</span> mean?","‘(It) might break.’"]]},{"id":"cannot","n":"5.14","title":"Can't: walya with -langu","grammar":"Verbs › Possibility: -langu, p. 31","html":"<p>When <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span> is used with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> ‘not’, it means someone or something is <strong>not able</strong> to do something.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganga-langu</span><span class=\"g\">carry-possibility</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I cannot carry this.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 382/62</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganga-langu</span> on its own would mean ‘can carry’; with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> it means ‘cannot carry’.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> mean?","‘not’."],["How does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> change the meaning of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganga-langu</span>?","From ‘can carry’ to ‘cannot carry’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya ngathu nhanha-guru ganga-langu</span> mean?","‘I cannot carry this.’"]]},{"id":"verb-summary","n":"5.15","title":"Verb endings at a glance","grammar":"Verbs › Summary, p. 32","html":"<p>These three sentences are the same except for the verb ending:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span><span class=\"g\">man-dual</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3dl.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">run-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two men run away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 258/2</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span><span class=\"g\">man-dual</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3dl.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-nga</span><span class=\"g\">run-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two men ran away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 259/3</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span><span class=\"g\">man-dual</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3dl.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-rra</span><span class=\"g\">run-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two men will run away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 260/4</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Verb form</th><th>Ending</th><th>For actions or events that…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>present</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span></td><td>are happening now</td></tr><tr><td>past</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span></td><td>have already happened</td></tr><tr><td>future</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span></td><td>will happen, at any time in the future</td></tr><tr><td>irrealis</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span></td><td>might happen but aren't certain; things the speaker wishes would happen</td></tr><tr><td>factive</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lha</span></td><td>will certainly happen, or are about to happen</td></tr><tr><td>possibility</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langu</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span></td><td>are possible</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["Which ending puts a verb in the future?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula bula-guru guthi-nga</span> mean?","‘The two men ran away.’"],["Which ending would you use for something that might happen but isn't certain?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rri</span>."]]}]},{"n":6,"title":"Noun endings","intro":"<p>Kullilli shows what each noun is doing in a sentence with endings. You don't need to remember the names of the endings: they are just labels for the different jobs.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"what-is-a-noun","n":"6.1","title":"What is a noun?","grammar":"Nouns › What is a noun?, p. 33","html":"<p>A noun is a word that names something: usually people, places or things. Nouns can also name ideas, feelings, dreams and thoughts. This is true in every language. There are three groups:</p><ul><li><strong>Common nouns</strong>: general words for people, places and things (person, dog, tree, food, house, anger), such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna</span> ‘man’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi</span> ‘dog’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra</span> ‘camp’.</li><li><strong>Proper nouns</strong>: names, which in English usually start with a capital letter (Australia, Kullilli, March).</li><li><strong>Pronouns</strong>: words used instead of nouns (you, she, he, them, us, it). They have their own units.</li></ul>","qa":[["What does a noun do?","It names something: a person, place, thing, or an idea or feeling."],["What are the three groups of nouns?","Common nouns, proper nouns and pronouns."],["Is ‘anger’ a noun?","Yes (a common noun naming a feeling)."]]},{"id":"case-intro","n":"6.2","title":"Endings show who does what","grammar":"Nouns › Noun Suffixes, p. 33","html":"<p>Take the English sentence <em>The child saw the dog</em>. We know the child did the seeing because in English the doer usually comes before the verb, and the one it's done to comes after. Swap them (<em>The dog saw the child</em>) and the meaning changes.</p><p>Kullilli shows each noun's role with an <strong>ending</strong> on the noun. Linguists call this <em>case</em>. So word order is not the main way Kullilli shows who does what. For example, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> on a noun shows that it is the doer:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mara</span><span class=\"g\">hand</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhapa-gala</span><span class=\"g\">lick-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">me.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-andru</span><span class=\"g\">dog-ergative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The dog is licking my hand.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 485/37</p></figure><p>Unlike English, the doer here comes at the end of the sentence, after the verb.</p>","qa":[["How does English show who did what in <em>The child saw the dog</em>?","By word order: the doer comes before the verb."],["How does Kullilli show it?","With endings on the nouns (case)."],["In 485/37, who is doing the licking, and how can you tell?","The dog: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-andru</span> has the doer ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span>."]]},{"id":"nominative-ani","n":"6.3","title":"The only noun: -ani","grammar":"Nouns › Nominative, p. 34","html":"<p>Take the English sentence <em>The dog is good</em>. It has only one noun, <em>the dog</em>: a subject, but no object (no noun that has something done to it). In a sentence like this, the noun is in the <em>nominative</em> case, which Kullilli marks with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span><span class=\"g\">it.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">dog-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali</span><span class=\"g\">good</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘This dog is good.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 76/12</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-gala</span><span class=\"g\">rise-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mirkirinyi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">moon-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The moon is coming up.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 319/63</p></figure>","qa":[["When is a noun in the nominative case?","When it is the subject of a sentence with no object."],["What is the nominative ending?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span> (or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span>; see the next lesson)."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-gala mirkirinyi-ani</span> mean?","‘The moon is coming up.’"]]},{"id":"nominative-ni","n":"6.4","title":"-ni after a","grammar":"Nouns › Nominative, p. 34","html":"<p>The grammar says that when a word already ends in <strong>a</strong>, the nominative ending is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span> instead of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span>. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwa</span> ‘child’ ends in <em>a</em>, so it takes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span>:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganguri-gala</span><span class=\"g\">cry-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwa-ni</span><span class=\"g\">child-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That child is crying.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 152/24</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">water-locative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru</span><span class=\"g\">them-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwa-ni</span><span class=\"g\">child-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">winta-gala</span><span class=\"g\">swim-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The kids are all swimming in the water.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 141/13</p></figure><p>This sentence has two nouns, the kids and the water, but the kids aren't doing anything <strong>to</strong> the water: they are <strong>in</strong> it. So <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwa</span> still takes the nominative ending. (Talking about places has its own lessons.)</p><aside class=\"note\"><p>You will also see <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span> after words ending in <em>a</em> in some recorded sentences, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-ani</span> ‘man’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-ani</span> ‘water’.</p></aside>","qa":[["Which form of the nominative goes on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwa</span> ‘child’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span>: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwa-ni</span>."],["<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walga</span> means ‘woman’. What is its nominative form?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walga-ni</span>."],["Why don't the kids in 141/13 take a doer ending like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span>?","They aren't doing anything to the water; they are in it."]]},{"id":"ergative-andru","n":"6.5","title":"Doing something to something: -andru","grammar":"Nouns › Ergative, p. 34–35","html":"<p>The <em>ergative</em> case is used for a subject when the sentence <strong>also has an object</strong>. In <em>The dog saw the child</em>, the dog does something to something else (the child), so in Kullilli it takes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span>:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-andru</span><span class=\"g\">dog-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-nga</span><span class=\"g\">see-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">waliwa-nha</span><span class=\"g\">child-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The dog saw the child.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gubi-andru</span><span class=\"g\">doctor-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali-munka-rra</span><span class=\"g\">good-causative-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The doctor will heal it.’</figcaption><p class=\"lit\">Literally: ‘The doctor will make it good.’</p><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 469/21</p></figure>","qa":[["When does a subject take <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span>?","When the sentence also has an object."],["What's the difference between <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-ani</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-andru</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-ani</span>: the dog is the only noun (no object). <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-andru</span>: the dog is doing something to something else."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gubi-andru nhanha-guru dhali-munka-rra</span> mean?","‘The doctor will heal it’ (literally ‘will make it good’)."]]},{"id":"ergative-lu","n":"6.6","title":"Another doer ending: -lu","grammar":"Nouns › Ergative, p. 35","html":"<p>Another ending used the same way is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lu</span>. But we only have examples of it with the word for ‘uncle’, the word for ‘who’, and the number ‘two’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawalya-lu</span><span class=\"g\">uncle-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marnnga-gala</span><span class=\"g\">make-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Uncle is making a boomerang.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 99/35</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-lu</span><span class=\"g\">who-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-nga</span><span class=\"g\">grasp-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhangala</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-nha</span><span class=\"g\">stick-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Who picked up my stick here?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 111/47</p></figure><p>The recorded sentences don't clearly show the difference between <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lu</span>. It might have to do with gender: some other Aboriginal languages, like Diyari, have case endings that depend on gender, so <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lu</span> might be an optional ending for male nouns. We can't be sure.</p>","qa":[["Which three words are <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lu</span> found with?","‘uncle’, ‘who’ and ‘two’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawalya-lu marnnga-gala bakaranyi</span> mean?","‘Uncle is making a boomerang.’"],["Do we know for sure why there are two doer endings?","No. It might have to do with gender, as in Diyari, but we can't be sure."]]},{"id":"accusative-anha","n":"6.7","title":"Having something done to it: -anha","grammar":"Nouns › Talking about things that have an action done to them (accusative), p. 35–36","html":"<p>In a sentence with two nouns, one does the action and the other has the action done to it. The second one is in the <em>accusative</em> case, marked with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anha</span><span class=\"g\">meat-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">we-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhtha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">eat-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘All of us will eat the meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 13</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">you.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-gala</span><span class=\"g\">see-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaldra-anha</span><span class=\"g\">kangaroo-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You see the kangaroo.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 33</p></figure><p>Word order doesn't decide who does what. In 13 the noun with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span> comes before the verb; in 33 it comes after. Either way, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span> tells us it is the one the action is done to.</p><aside class=\"note\"><p>The grammar says that, unlike <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span> doesn't shorten after a word ending in <em>a</em>. But you will see <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> in some recorded sentences, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-nha</span> ‘stick’.</p></aside>","qa":[["Which ending marks the noun that has the action done to it?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span> (accusative)."],["In 13, does the noun with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span> come before or after the verb?","Before the verb."],["How do you know <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaldra-anha</span> is what is being seen, wherever it is in the sentence?","Because it has the accusative ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span>."]]},{"id":"dative-anga","n":"6.8","title":"To someone, for someone: -anga","grammar":"Nouns › Talking about a noun receiving something or benefitting from an action (dative), p. 36–37","html":"<p>A noun that receives something, or that an action is done for, is in the <em>dative</em> case, marked with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span> (sometimes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span>). It is like English <em>to (someone)</em> or <em>for (someone or something)</em>: <em>The child gave a toy to the dog.</em></p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanki-nga</span><span class=\"g\">find-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span><span class=\"g\">unable</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanki-nga</span><span class=\"g\">find-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span><span class=\"g\">his</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi-anga</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang-dative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I looked, I couldn't find his boomerang.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 342/22</p></figure>","qa":[["Which English words are like the dative ending?","‘to’ and ‘for’."],["What is the dative ending?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span> (sometimes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span>)."],["Which word in 342/22 has the dative ending?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi-anga</span> ‘boomerang’."]]},{"id":"dative-goal","n":"6.9","title":"Going to, going for: -anga","grammar":"Nouns › dative, p. 37","html":"<p>The dative can also show the <strong>goal</strong> of an action: where someone is going.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go.back-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra-anga</span><span class=\"g\">camp-dative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I'm going back to my camp now.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 7</p></figure><p>It can also show a <strong>purpose</strong>: what someone is going for.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anga</span><span class=\"g\">meat-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-guru</span><span class=\"g\">them.two-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of them are going for meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 130/2</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra-anga</span> mean in 7?","‘to the camp’."],["How do you say ‘going for meat’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anga … yanhtha-gala</span>."],["What two kinds of goal can the dative show?","A place someone is going to, and a purpose (what they're going for)."]]},{"id":"locative","n":"6.10","title":"At, in, on: -langa, -lunga","grammar":"Nouns › Talking about where something is (locative), p. 37–38","html":"<p>To say where something is, English uses <em>at</em>, <em>in</em> and <em>on</em>. Kullilli uses the <em>locative</em> endings <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span>. There is no clear difference between them in the recorded sentences; <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span> is used more often.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">sit-imperative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra-langa</span><span class=\"g\">camp-locative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Sit down in my camp.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 161/33</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span><span class=\"g\">OK</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhangala-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.loc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gali-nga</span><span class=\"g\">put-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">daka-langa</span><span class=\"g\">ground-locative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘OK, I put (it) over there on the ground.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 357/37</p></figure>","qa":[["What are the two locative endings?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span>."],["How do you say ‘in my camp’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya ngurra-langa</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">daka-langa</span> mean?","‘on the ground’."]]},{"id":"locative-time-company","n":"6.11","title":"At night, with the dog: more uses of -langa","grammar":"Nouns › locative, p. 38","html":"<p>The locative can also say <strong>when</strong> something happens:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span><span class=\"g\">yesterday</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span><span class=\"g\">all</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">dog-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngawu-langa</span><span class=\"g\">night-locative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandrangala</span><span class=\"g\">us.loc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-dhika-gali</span><span class=\"g\">go-go.back-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Yesterday those dogs came up to us at night.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 455/7</p></figure><p>And it can mean ‘in company with, with’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.loc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">birkibirki-wa</span><span class=\"g\">play.play-emphatic</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">good-locative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">dog-locative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That one down there is playing with the good dog.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 540/26</p></figure><p>Both words for ‘the good dog’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali</span> ‘good’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi</span> ‘dog’, have <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span>.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘at night’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngawu-langa</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali-lunga dhithi-lunga</span> mean?","‘with the good dog’."],["Name two meanings of the locative besides ‘at, in, on’.","A time (‘at night’) and company (‘with’)."]]},{"id":"ablative","n":"6.12","title":"From: -andru","grammar":"Nouns › Talking about movement away from something (ablative), p. 38–39","html":"<p>The endings <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ndru</span> on a noun show movement <strong>away from</strong> it. This is called the <em>ablative</em> case. It sounds the same as the ergative <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span>, but it does a different job.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span><span class=\"g\">his</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra-andru</span><span class=\"g\">camp-ablative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidya-ni</span><span class=\"g\">father-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go.back-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My father comes back from his camp.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 59</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buli-nga</span><span class=\"g\">fall-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burngu-andru</span><span class=\"g\">gum.tree-ablative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span><span class=\"g\">it.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That one fell down from that gum tree.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 64</p></figure><p>The ablative is also how you say where someone is from: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-nuga-andru</span> ‘from the big water’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">Brisbane-andru</span> ‘from Brisbane’.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> mean on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">burngu</span> ‘gum tree’ in 64?","‘from’."],["How can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> mean both ‘doer’ and ‘from’?","They are two different endings that sound the same; the rest of the sentence shows which one it is."],["How do you say ‘from his camp’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka ngurra-andru</span>."]]},{"id":"instrumental","n":"6.13","title":"With a tool: -andru","grammar":"Nouns › Talking about using something (instrumental), p. 39","html":"<p>The same-sounding ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> (or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ndru</span>) also shows the <strong>tool</strong> or thing used to do something. This is the <em>instrumental</em> case, like English <em>with</em> in <em>The child ate with a spoon</em>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nampa</span><span class=\"g\">cover.up</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">it.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaldra-anha</span><span class=\"g\">kangaroo-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dulpa-andru</span><span class=\"g\">ashes-instrumental</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Cover up this kangaroo with ashes.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 348/28</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-iyi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">strike-reflexive-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">he.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">daranya-andru</span><span class=\"g\">axe-instrumental</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘He hit himself with a stone axe.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 86/22</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘with ashes’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dulpa-andru</span>."],["What does the instrumental case show?","The tool or thing used to do something."],["How many different jobs does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> do?","Three: the doer (ergative), ‘from’ (ablative) and ‘with a tool’ (instrumental)."]]},{"id":"case-summary","n":"6.14","title":"Noun endings at a glance","grammar":"Nouns › Summary of Kullilli Cases &amp; Noun Endings, p. 40","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Ending</th><th>For nouns that are…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>nominative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span></td><td>the subject of a sentence with no object</td></tr><tr><td>ergative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lu</span></td><td>the subject of a sentence with an object</td></tr><tr><td>accusative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span></td><td>the object: the one the action is done to</td></tr><tr><td>dative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span></td><td>the goal, receiver or beneficiary of an action</td></tr><tr><td>locative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span></td><td>the place where something happens</td></tr><tr><td>ablative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span></td><td>the place something moves away from</td></tr><tr><td>instrumental</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span></td><td>the tool used to do something</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>The nominative is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ani</span> in most cases, but the grammar gives <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span> for nouns ending in <em>a</em>.</li><li>The ergative is usually <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> but sometimes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lu</span>, which might have to do with gender.</li><li>The dative has several forms: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-angani</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngani</span>. We're not sure yet how this works.</li></ul>","qa":[["Which ending would you put on a tool?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-andru</span> (instrumental)."],["Which endings show where something happens?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span> (locative)."],["Name the forms of the dative ending.","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-angani</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngani</span>."]]}]},{"n":7,"title":"Pronouns: I, you, they","intro":"<p>Pronouns are words that stand for a person, animal, place or thing. Kullilli pronouns change with <strong>person</strong> (I, you, or someone else), <strong>number</strong> (one, two, or more) and <strong>case</strong> (the job the pronoun does, as with the noun endings).</p>","lessons":[{"id":"i-and-me","n":"7.1","title":"I and me: nganyi, ngathu, nganha","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Singular, p. 41–42","html":"<p>English has <em>I</em> for the doer or feeler (the <em>subject</em>: <em>I saw the dog</em>, <em>I am hungry</em>) and <em>me</em> for the one something is done to (the <em>object</em>: <em>The dog saw me</em>). Kullilli has this difference too, but it has <strong>two</strong> words for ‘I’.</p><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> ‘I’ is used with verbs that have no object, and with verbs ending in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> (doing something to yourself). This is the <em>nominative</em> ‘I’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba-rra</span><span class=\"g\">go-away-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I am going away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 180/52</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-iyi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">strike-reflexive-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I hit myself.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 87/23</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span> ‘I’ is used with verbs that have an object. This is the <em>ergative</em> ‘I’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhupu-anha</span><span class=\"g\">smoke-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wupi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">smell-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I smell smoke.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 346/26</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span> ‘me’ is the object, the <em>accusative</em>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala-ba</span><span class=\"g\">answer-away</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Answer me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 314/58</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> ‘I’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span> ‘I’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span> ‘me’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["Which ‘I’ do you use with a verb that has an object?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span>."],["What is the Kullilli for ‘me’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span>."],["Which ‘I’ is used in ‘I am going away’, and why?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span>, because the verb has no object."]]},{"id":"you-one","n":"7.2","title":"You (one person): ini, yundru, inha","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Singular, p. 42–43","html":"<p>English has one word, <em>you</em>, for all of these jobs. Kullilli has three words for ‘you’ (talking to one person):</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba</span><span class=\"g\">go-away</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You go on.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 459/11</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gapa-rra</span><span class=\"g\">follow-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You will follow me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 380/60</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span><span class=\"g\">OK</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bura-nga</span><span class=\"g\">tell-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘OK, I told you.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 220/28</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span> ‘you’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span> ‘you’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span> ‘you’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["Which ‘you’ is the object?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span>."],["Which ‘you’ is used in ‘You will follow me’, and why?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span>, because ‘follow’ has an object (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span> ‘me’)."],["How many words for ‘you’ (one person) have you met in this lesson?","Three: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span>."]]},{"id":"i-ask-you","n":"7.3","title":"I ask you, you ask me","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Singular, p. 43","html":"<p>Now we can put ‘I’ and ‘you’ together. The verb <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka</span> ‘ask’ has an object, so the subject must be ergative, not nominative.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gala</span><span class=\"g\">ask-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I ask you.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 310/54</p></figure><p>The other way round:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gala</span><span class=\"g\">ask-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You ask me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘You ask me’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gala yundru nganha</span>."],["Why is ‘I’ <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span> and not <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> in ‘I ask you’?","Because ‘ask’ has an object, so the subject is ergative."],["Which word is the object in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gala ngathu inha</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span> ‘you’."]]},{"id":"he-she-it","n":"7.4","title":"He, she, it: nhani, nhandru, nhanha","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Singular, p. 43–44","html":"<p>English uses <em>he</em> and <em>she</em> for people and <em>it</em> for things. Kullilli doesn't make these differences: one set of words is used for any person or thing, male or female, living or not. You just need the right subject or object form.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gari-gala</span><span class=\"g\">burn-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘It isn't burning.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 16</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">girni-anha</span><span class=\"g\">strong-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhandru-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.erg-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gatyi-li-rra</span><span class=\"g\">break-apart-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘He will break apart the strong (thing).’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 280/24</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wirnbi-dhika</span><span class=\"g\">throw-back</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Throw it away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 17</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span> ‘he, she, it, they (one person)’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhandru</span> ‘he, she, it, they’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span> ‘him, her, it, them’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["Does Kullilli have different words for ‘he’ and ‘she’?","No. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span> (and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhandru</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span>) is used for any person or thing."],["Which form is used in ‘Throw it away’, and why?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span>, because ‘it’ is the object."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani walya gari-gala</span> mean?","‘It isn't burning.’"]]},{"id":"masculine-pronouns","n":"7.5","title":"Words just for men: nhina, nhulu, nhinha","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Singular, p. 44–45","html":"<p>Kullilli also has third-person pronouns that refer only to males: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhia</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhina</span> (nominative), <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhulu</span> (ergative) and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span> (object). The sources suggest they weren't used very often, and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhandru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span> can refer to males too. Perhaps they were going out of use, the way <em>thee</em> and <em>thou</em> have in English.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span><span class=\"g\">what-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhina</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">laugh-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Why (is he) laughing?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 327/7</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhulu</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gala-ba-nga</span><span class=\"g\">answer-away-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘He didn't answer me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 313/57</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-nga</span><span class=\"g\">ask-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I asked him.’</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhia</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhina</span> ‘he’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhulu</span> ‘he’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span> ‘him’</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["Which of these pronouns is the object form?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span> ‘him’."],["Do you have to use these words when talking about a man?","No. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhandru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span> can refer to men too."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-nga ngathu nhinha</span> mean?","‘I asked him.’"]]},{"id":"singular-patterns","n":"7.6","title":"Patterns in the singular pronouns","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Singular, p. 45","html":"<p>Here are the nine main singular subject and object pronouns:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st (I, me)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nyi</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-thu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nha</span></td></tr><tr><td>2nd (you)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ni</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yu-ndru</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-nha</span></td></tr><tr><td>3rd (he, she, it)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-ni</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-ndru</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Some patterns help you remember them:</p><ul><li>All the first-person forms start with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-</span>, and all the third-person forms start with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-</span>.</li><li>The second-person forms start with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-</span>, except the ergative <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span>.</li><li>The second and third person have the same endings: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span> (nominative), <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ndru</span> (ergative), <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> (accusative). The first-person endings are similar but not quite the same.</li></ul><p>Linguists call sets of endings like these <em>declensions</em>.</p>","qa":[["What do all the third-person singular pronouns start with?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-</span>."],["Which second-person form breaks the <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-</span> pattern?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span>."],["Which ending do all the singular object (accusative) pronouns share?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span>."]]},{"id":"we-two","n":"7.7","title":"The two of us: ngali","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Dual, p. 46","html":"<p>English has <em>we</em>, <em>you</em> and <em>they</em> for more than one person. Kullilli also has special <strong>dual</strong> pronouns for exactly <strong>two</strong> people or things: ‘the two of us’, ‘the two of you’, ‘the two of them’. For pairs, you use these instead of the plural pronouns.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">murta-iyi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">hurt-reflexive-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span><span class=\"g\">1dl.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of us feel sick.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 43</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyi-nha</span><span class=\"g\">fire-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span><span class=\"g\">think</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">1dl-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulpa-rra</span><span class=\"g\">light-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I think we (the two of us) will light a fire.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 151/23</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-rra</span><span class=\"g\">give-imperative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-nha</span><span class=\"g\">1dl-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Give to the two of us.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 281/25</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["What does <em>dual</em> mean?","Exactly two."],["How do you say ‘the two of us’ as the object?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-nha</span>."],["Which form is used in 151/23, where the two of us light a fire?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-ngu</span>, the ergative, because ‘light’ has an object (the fire)."]]},{"id":"you-two","n":"7.8","title":"The two of you: yula","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Dual, p. 46–47","html":"<figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span><span class=\"g\">2dl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhina-ba-rra</span><span class=\"g\">stand.up-away-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of you stand up.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 241/49</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">2dl-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi</span><span class=\"g\">meat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nuga</span><span class=\"g\">big</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-rra</span><span class=\"g\">give-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of you will give me much meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 74/10</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-nha</span><span class=\"g\">2dl-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-gala</span><span class=\"g\">give-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I give it to you (the two of you).’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 71/7</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["What is the ergative of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngu</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula dhina-ba-rra</span> mean?","‘The two of you stand up.’"],["How do you say ‘the two of you’ as the object?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-nha</span>."]]},{"id":"they-two","n":"7.9","title":"The two of them: bula","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Dual, p. 47–48","html":"<figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ba</span><span class=\"g\">3dl.nom-emphatic</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guri</span><span class=\"g\">this.way</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of them (are coming) this way.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 81/17</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">murra</span><span class=\"g\">finished</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-guru-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">3dl-there-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">muraba-nga</span><span class=\"g\">finish-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunga-anha</span><span class=\"g\">food-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘OK, the two of them have finished the food.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 4</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguta-la-rra-ba</span><span class=\"g\">give-?-imperative-emphatic</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-nha</span><span class=\"g\">3dl-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Give to the two of them.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 285/29</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-bula</span> can also go on a noun: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna</span> ‘man’ becomes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span> ‘two men’. It can go on a describing word too:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span><span class=\"g\">man-dual</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garlgugarlgu-bula</span><span class=\"g\">weak.weak-dual</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two men are weak.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 215/23</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>(A question mark in the English line means we don't know what that part means.)</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘two men’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span>."],["What is the object form of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-nha</span>."],["In 215/23, which two words have <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-bula</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span> ‘two men’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garlgugarlgu-bula</span> ‘weak (two)’."]]},{"id":"dual-pattern","n":"7.10","title":"The dual pattern","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Dual, p. 48","html":"<p>The dual pronouns are simpler than the singular ones. Start with the nominative, add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngu</span> for the ergative and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> for the accusative. There are no irregular forms.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st (the two of us)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-nha</span></td></tr><tr><td>2nd (the two of you)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-nha</span></td></tr><tr><td>3rd (the two of them)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["How do you make the ergative of a dual pronoun?","Add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngu</span> to the nominative."],["How do you make the accusative of a dual pronoun?","Add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> to the nominative."],["Are there any irregular dual pronouns?","No."]]},{"id":"we-all","n":"7.11","title":"We all: ngandra","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Plural, p. 48–49","html":"<p><em>Plural</em> pronouns are for <strong>three or more</strong>. In English: <em>we</em>, <em>you all</em> (<em>youse</em>, <em>you guys</em>, <em>you lot</em>) and <em>they</em>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaymari-nga</span><span class=\"g\">thirsty-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra</span><span class=\"g\">1pl.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘We are thirsty.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 32</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nanpa-rra</span><span class=\"g\">bury-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">1pl-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘We will bury him.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 466/18</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-dhika-ri</span><span class=\"g\">give-back-irrealis</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-nha</span><span class=\"g\">1pl-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘(You might) give back to us all.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 283/27</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["How many people do plural pronouns refer to?","Three or more."],["How do you say ‘we’ (all of us) as the subject of a verb with an object?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngu</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaymari-nga ngandra</span> mean?","‘We are thirsty.’"]]},{"id":"you-all","n":"7.12","title":"You all: yurra","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Plural, p. 49","html":"<figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span><span class=\"g\">2pl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">muruba-nga</span><span class=\"g\">go-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You all are finished.’</figcaption><p class=\"lit\">Literally: ‘You all have gone.’</p><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 5</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">2pl-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhtha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">eat-purpose</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You (all) eat meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 72/8</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anha</span><span class=\"g\">meat-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-nha</span><span class=\"g\">2pl-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-gala</span><span class=\"g\">give-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I give meat to you (all) to eat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 72/8</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["How do you say ‘you all’ as the object?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-nha</span>."],["What is the nominative ‘you all’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span>."],["In the last example, who gives the meat?","‘I’ (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span>)."]]},{"id":"they-all","n":"7.13","title":"They all: dhana","grammar":"Pronouns › Subjects and Objects – Plural, p. 50","html":"<figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhuna-wira</span><span class=\"g\">time-wait</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu-nu</span><span class=\"g\">soon-?</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-na-rra</span><span class=\"g\">come-?-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Wait a while, soon they will all come past.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 390/6</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-there-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">muraba-nga</span><span class=\"g\">finish-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunga-anha</span><span class=\"g\">food-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘All of them have finished this food.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 1</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-la-rra-ba</span><span class=\"g\">give-?-future-emphatic</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-nha</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Give to them all.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 286/30</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Just like the dual, every plural pronoun adds <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngu</span> for the ergative and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> for the accusative:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Subject (nominative)</th><th>Subject (ergative)</th><th>Object (accusative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st (we all)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-nha</span></td></tr><tr><td>2nd (you all)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-nha</span></td></tr><tr><td>3rd (they all)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-nha</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["What is the ergative of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngu</span>."],["How do you say ‘Give to them all’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-la-rra-ba dhana-nha</span>."],["Do the plural pronouns follow the same pattern as the dual?","Yes: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngu</span> for the ergative and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> for the accusative."]]},{"id":"plural-buka","n":"7.14","title":"Showing plural with buka","grammar":"Pronouns › Another way to show plural nouns, p. 50–51","html":"<p>Another way to show that a noun is plural is the word <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> ‘all’. You wouldn't say ‘all’ about one or two things, so if <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> is there, the noun must mean three or more, even without a plural pronoun nearby.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-ani</span><span class=\"g\">man-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garlgugarlgu-ani</span><span class=\"g\">weak.weak-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span><span class=\"g\">all</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The men are all weak.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 214/22</p></figure><p>The sentence only says <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna</span> ‘man’, but because <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> is there we know it means more than two men. You can also use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> ‘many, a lot’ to talk about plural nouns.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> tell you about a noun?","That it is plural (three or more)."],["In 214/22, how do we know it means ‘men’ and not ‘man’?","Because of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span> ‘all’ (and the plural pronoun <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru</span>)."],["Which other word can show that there are many?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> ‘many, a lot’."]]}]},{"n":8,"title":"Pronouns: my, to me, from me","intro":"<p>Pronouns can take the other case endings too: the dative, locative and ablative. With pronouns the meanings can be a little different.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"possessive-pronouns","n":"8.1","title":"My, your, his: possessive pronouns","grammar":"Pronouns › Dative Pronouns, p. 51–52","html":"<p>With pronouns, the dative means <strong>possession</strong>, like English <em>my, mine, our, your, his, her, their</em>.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Singular</th><th>Dual</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nytya</span> ‘my’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ngani</span> ‘our (two)’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngani</span> ‘our (all)’</td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ngka</span> ‘your’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngani</span> ‘your (two)’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngani</span> ‘your (all)’</td></tr><tr><td>3rd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-ngka</span> ‘his, her, its’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngani</span> ‘their (two)’</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngani</span> ‘their (all)’</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The dual and plural forms all end in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngani</span>. The second- and third-person singular end in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngka</span>, and ‘my’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span>.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya ngarnidya</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘my father’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka dhithi</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘your dog’</span></li></ul><p>There is much more on possession in the unit on owning and belonging.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘your’ (talking to one person)?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span>."],["Which ending do all the dual and plural possessive pronouns have?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngani</span>."],["How do you say ‘our’ (three or more of us)?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngani</span>."]]},{"id":"locative-pronouns","n":"8.2","title":"To me, to you: -ngala","grammar":"Pronouns › Locative Pronouns, p. 52–54","html":"<p>With pronouns, the locative shows the <strong>goal</strong> of an action: who or what it goes towards, like <em>me</em> in <em>Speak to me</em>. English uses <em>me</em> for both ‘me’ the object and ‘to me’, but Kullilli has different words. These pronouns end in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngala</span>, except ‘to me’, which is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganala</span>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">speak-imperative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganala</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.loc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Speak to me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 247/55</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha</span><span class=\"g\">speak</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngalangala</span><span class=\"g\">1dl.loc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Speak to the two of us.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 249/57</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawa</span><span class=\"g\">come</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra</span><span class=\"g\">1pl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">speak-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala-burlu</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc-over.there</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Come on, we will talk to that one over there.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 253/61</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Singular</th><th>Dual</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngala</span></td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngala</span>*</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngala</span></td></tr><tr><td>3rd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngala</span>*</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngala</span>*</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>* These forms aren't found in the sources. They have been worked out from the regular pattern.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘Speak to me’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-rra nganala</span>."],["What ending do most of these pronouns have?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngala</span>."],["What does a star (*) in the table mean?","The form isn't found in the sources; it was worked out from the pattern."]]},{"id":"ablative-pronouns","n":"8.3","title":"Away from me: -ngura","grammar":"Pronouns › Ablative Pronouns, p. 54","html":"<p>Like ablative nouns, ablative pronouns show what an action moves <strong>away from</strong>. They end in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngura</span>, except ‘from me’, which is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganura</span>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba</span><span class=\"g\">go-away</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganura</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.abl</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Go away from me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 453/5</p></figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Singular</th><th>Dual</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nura</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ngura</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ngura</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngura</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>3rd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-ngura</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngura</span>*</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngura</span>*</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>* Not found in the sources; worked out from the pattern.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘Go away from me’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba nganura</span>."],["What is ‘away from you all’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngura</span>."],["Which form breaks the <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngura</span> pattern?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganura</span> ‘from me’."]]},{"id":"pronoun-patterns","n":"8.4","title":"How the pronouns fit together","grammar":"Pronouns › Summary on Kullilli personal pronouns, p. 54–55","html":"<p>Which pronoun you use depends on three things:</p><ul><li><strong>person</strong>: first, second or third</li><li><strong>number</strong>: singular, dual or plural</li><li><strong>case</strong>: nominative, ergative, accusative, dative, locative or ablative</li></ul><p>That makes 54 pronouns (more with the masculine ones), but there are patterns. Most pronouns are a root plus a case ending. These are the roots (where there are two, which one is used depends on the case):</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Singular</th><th>Dual</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-</span></td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yu-</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-</span></td></tr><tr><td>3rd</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The endings come in three sets: one for ‘I’, one for ‘you’ and ‘he, she, it’, and one for all the dual and plural pronouns.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Nominative</th><th>Ergative</th><th>Accusative</th><th>Dative</th><th>Locative</th><th>Ablative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1st person singular</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nyi</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-thu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nytya</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nura</span></td></tr><tr><td>2nd and 3rd singular</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ni</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ndru</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngka</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>all dual and plural</td><td>(none)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngani</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngura</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["What three things decide which pronoun to use?","Person, number and case."],["Using the tables, what is the locative of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngala</span>."],["Which ending is the same in all three sets?","The accusative <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span> (the locative and ablative are also nearly the same)."]]},{"id":"pronoun-table","n":"8.5","title":"All the pronouns","grammar":"Pronouns › Summary on Kullilli personal pronouns, p. 56","html":"<p>Here are all 54 personal pronouns in one table, for reference.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Nominative</th><th>Ergative</th><th>Accusative</th><th>Dative</th><th>Locative</th><th>Ablative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nyi</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-thu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nytya</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nga-nura</span></td></tr><tr><td>you (one)</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ni</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yu-ndru</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ngka</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">i-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>he, she, it</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-ni</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-ndru</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nha-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-ngka</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>we two</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ngani</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>you two</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngani</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>they two</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngani</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bula-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>we all</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngani</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>you all</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngani</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yurra-ngura</span></td></tr><tr><td>they all</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngu</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-nha</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngani</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngala</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-ngura</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The forms with irregular roots are in the singular rows (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yu-</span> in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-</span> in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span>) and the dual ‘we two’ row (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-</span> in the dative, locative and ablative).</p>","qa":[["What is the dative of ‘you two’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-ngani</span>."],["Which root does ‘he, she, it’ use in the dative, locative and ablative?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhu-</span> (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungura</span>)."],["What is the ergative of ‘we all’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra-ngu</span>."]]}]},{"n":9,"title":"Question words","intro":"<p>Words for asking <em>who?</em>, <em>what?</em>, <em>why?</em> and <em>where?</em>. Like nouns and pronouns, they take case endings that depend on their job in the sentence.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"who","n":"9.1","title":"Who? wara-ni, wara-lu","grammar":"Pronouns › Question Pronouns › Human interrogative pronouns (“who”), p. 56–58","html":"<p>The word for ‘who’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara</span>, and it takes different endings, just as English has <em>who</em>, <em>whom</em> and <em>whose</em>.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>nominative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-ni</span></td></tr><tr><td>ergative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-lu</span></td></tr><tr><td>accusative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nha</span></td></tr><tr><td>dative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nga</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>‘Who ran away?’ has a doer but nothing that is done to, so ‘who’ is nominative:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-ni</span><span class=\"g\">who-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-nga</span><span class=\"g\">run-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Who ran away?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure><p>Picking something up is done <strong>to</strong> something (the stick), so ‘who’ is ergative:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-lu</span><span class=\"g\">who-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-nga</span><span class=\"g\">grasp-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhangala</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-nha</span><span class=\"g\">stick-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Who picked up my stick here?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 111/47</p></figure>","qa":[["What is the base word for ‘who’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara</span>."],["Which ‘who’ is used in ‘Who picked up my stick?’, and why?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-lu</span>, the ergative, because picking up is done to something (the stick)."],["How do you say ‘Who ran away?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-ni nhani-guru guthi-nga</span>."]]},{"id":"whom-whose","n":"9.2","title":"Who is that? Whose?","grammar":"Pronouns › Question Pronouns › Human interrogative pronouns (“who”), p. 58","html":"<p>The accusative <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nha</span> is used in questions like ‘Who is that man?’. Think of the answer, <em>That man is ____</em>: the blank is a bit like the object of <em>is</em>, so ‘who’ takes the accusative.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nha</span><span class=\"g\">who-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhia-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garni-ya</span><span class=\"g\">man-there(?)</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Who is that man?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 335/15</p></figure><p>The dative <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nga</span> means ‘whose’, asking who something belongs to:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nga</span><span class=\"g\">who-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">dog-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-dhika-dhika-gala</span><span class=\"g\">run-go.back-go.back-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Whose is this dog running around?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 94/30</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘whose’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nga</span>."],["Which form of ‘who’ is used in ‘Who is that man?’","The accusative, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nha</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-nga nhani dhithi-ani…</span> ask?","Whose dog this is."]]},{"id":"what","n":"9.3","title":"What? minha","grammar":"Pronouns › Question Pronouns › Non-human interrogative pronouns (“what”), p. 58–59","html":"<p>The base word for ‘what’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina</span>. For an ordinary ‘what’ question, Kullilli uses the accusative, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">minha</span> (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina</span> with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span>):</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">minha</span><span class=\"g\">what</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarinya-ni</span><span class=\"g\">name-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-yi</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-here</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">marni-ani</span><span class=\"g\">ceremonial.dance-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘What is the name of this dance?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 489/41</p></figure><p>The answer would be <em>The name of this dance is ____</em>, and as with ‘who’, the blank is a bit like the object of <em>is</em>.</p><p>There is also <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wani</span> ‘what’, but it has only been found in one phrase, ‘What's your name?’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wani</span><span class=\"g\">what</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarinya-ani</span><span class=\"g\">name-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘What's your name?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 332/12</p></figure>","qa":[["What is the usual word for ‘what?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">minha</span>."],["Which two parts make up <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">minha</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina</span> ‘what’ and the accusative ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nha</span>."],["Where is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wani</span> used?","Only in the question ‘What's your name?’ (<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wani ingka yarinya-ani</span>)."]]},{"id":"why","n":"9.4","title":"Why? mina-anga","grammar":"Pronouns › Question Pronouns › Non-human interrogative pronouns (“what”), p. 59","html":"<p>‘Why’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina</span> ‘what’ with the dative ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span>: literally ‘what for?’. The dative is used for the receiver or purpose of an action; here the ‘purpose’ is the reason, so <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span> means ‘for what reason?’</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span><span class=\"g\">what-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhina</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">laugh-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Why (is he) laughing?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 327/7</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘why?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span> mean literally?","‘what for?’"],["Which ending turns <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina</span> ‘what’ into ‘why’?","The dative ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span>."]]},{"id":"where","n":"9.5","title":"Where? Where from? Where to?","grammar":"Pronouns › Question Pronouns › Place interrogative pronouns (“where”), p. 59–61","html":"<p>The base word for ‘where’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala</span>.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Form</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>nominative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ani</span></td><td>where?</td></tr><tr><td>ablative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru</span></td><td>where from?</td></tr><tr><td>dative</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-anga</span></td><td>where to?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ani</span><span class=\"g\">where-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra</span><span class=\"g\">camp</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where is your camp?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 10</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru</span><span class=\"g\">where-ablative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where are you from?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 133/5</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-anga</span><span class=\"g\">where-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where are you going?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 181/53</p></figure><p>These are useful when you meet someone for the first time (see Greetings).</p>","qa":[["How do you ask ‘Where is your camp?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-ani ingka ngurra</span>."],["Which ending makes ‘where to?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span> (dative): <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-anga</span>."],["Why does ‘where from?’ use the ablative?","Because the ablative is about movement away from somewhere."]]}]},{"n":10,"title":"This and that","intro":"<p>How to point things out: <em>this boomerang</em>, <em>that meat</em>. Words like these are called <em>demonstratives</em>.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"this-yi","n":"10.1","title":"This: -yi","grammar":"This and that, p. 62","html":"<p>In English you might point at a book near you and say <em>this book</em>, and at one further away and say <em>that book</em>. Kullilli does this with the endings <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span> ‘this’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span> ‘that’. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span> also means ‘here’, and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span> also means ‘there’.</p><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span> attaches to the pronouns you have already met, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span>:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-yi</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-here</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gatyi-gali-ani</span><span class=\"g\">break-past-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘This boomerang is broken.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 336/16</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yandra-anha</span><span class=\"g\">rock-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-yi</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-here</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-ba-rra</span><span class=\"g\">rise-emphatic-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I lift this stone.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 169/41</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">malyu</span><span class=\"g\">anger</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-gala</span><span class=\"g\">rise-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-yi</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-here</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-ani</span><span class=\"g\">man-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.dat</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘This man is angry with me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 230/38</p></figure>","qa":[["Which ending means ‘this’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span> attach to in these examples?","To pronouns, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi-ani nhani-yi gatyi-gali-ani</span> mean?","‘This boomerang is broken.’"]]},{"id":"that-guru","n":"10.2","title":"That: -guru","grammar":"This and that, p. 62–63","html":"<p>To say ‘that’, attach <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span>:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yamba-nyara-gala</span><span class=\"g\">fight-reciprocal-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walga-ni</span><span class=\"g\">woman-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span><span class=\"g\">all</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-there</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That group of women are having a fight.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 343/23</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunma</span><span class=\"g\">uncover</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anha</span><span class=\"g\">meat-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Uncover that meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 350/30</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span><span class=\"g\">OK</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">murlpa-nga</span><span class=\"g\">make-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You made that boomerang.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 353/33</p></figure><p>McDonald and Wurm translated <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span> as ‘here’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span> as ‘there’. The guide's authors think ‘this’ and ‘that’ fit them better.</p>","qa":[["Which ending means ‘that’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span>."],["How do you say ‘Uncover that meat’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunma nhanha-guru nguthi-anha</span>."],["How did McDonald and Wurm translate <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span>?","As ‘here’ and ‘there’."]]},{"id":"demonstrative-agreement","n":"10.3","title":"Matching the pronoun to the noun","grammar":"This and that, p. 63","html":"<p>When a pronoun with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-yi</span> goes with a noun, the pronoun has the <strong>same case</strong> as the noun. Linguists call this <em>agreement</em>.</p><p>In ‘Uncover that meat’, the meat has something done to it, so <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi</span> ‘meat’ has the accusative <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span>, and the pronoun is the accusative <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span> (‘it’), with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-guru</span> attached:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunma</span><span class=\"g\">uncover</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anha</span><span class=\"g\">meat-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Uncover that meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 350/30</p></figure><p>Sentence 353/33 (in the last lesson) is a bit different: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span> is accusative, but <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi</span> ‘boomerang’ has no ending.</p>","qa":[["In ‘Uncover that meat’, which two words are accusative?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anha</span>."],["What do linguists call this matching of cases?","Agreement."],["Which pronoun would go with a nominative noun ‘that dog’: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span>, because <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span> is the nominative."]]}]},{"n":11,"title":"Places and directions","intro":"<p>Saying where things are (over there, far away, close by) and which way actions go.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"place-locative","n":"11.1","title":"Where it happens: -lunga, -langa","grammar":"Locations and directions › Locations, p. 64; The locative on nouns, p. 67","html":"<p>Remember the locative endings <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span>, like English <em>in</em>, <em>at</em> or <em>on</em>. On a noun, they show the place where something happens.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buli-gali</span><span class=\"g\">fall-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">/</span><span class=\"g\">/</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mara</span><span class=\"g\">hand</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-iyi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">strike-reflexive-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">tree-locative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I fell and hit my hand on this stick.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 497/49</p></figure><p>The stick (literally ‘tree’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra</span>) is where the hand was hit, so <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra</span> takes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">sit-imperative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra-langa</span><span class=\"g\">camp-locative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Sit down in my camp.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 161/33</p></figure><p>The camp is where the sitting should happen.</p>","qa":[["What do <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-lunga</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-langa</span> show on a noun?","The place where something happens (‘in, at, on’)."],["In 497/49, where was the hand hit?","On the stick: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-lunga</span>."],["How do you say ‘in my camp’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya ngurra-langa</span>."]]},{"id":"burlu","n":"11.2","title":"Over there: -burlu","grammar":"Locations and directions › Locations, p. 65","html":"<p>The ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-burlu</span> goes on pronouns and means ‘down there, over there’. Here it is on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala</span> (‘to him, her, it’), making ‘that one over there’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawa</span><span class=\"g\">come</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra</span><span class=\"g\">1pl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">speak-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala-burlu</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc-over.there</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Come on, we will talk to that one over there.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 253/61</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-burlu</span> mean?","‘down there, over there’."],["What kind of word does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-burlu</span> attach to?","Pronouns."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala-burlu</span> mean in 253/61?","‘(to) that one over there’."]]},{"id":"galyi","n":"11.3","title":"Far away: galyi","grammar":"Locations and directions › Locations, p. 65","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span> is a word on its own meaning ‘far away’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">muna-manhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">?-get-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhangala</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span><span class=\"g\">far</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You will meet him over there, far away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 388/4</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span> mean?","‘far away’."],["Is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span> a word on its own or an ending?","A word on its own."],["What does 388/4 mean?","‘You will meet him over there, far away.’"]]},{"id":"gari-far","n":"11.4","title":"Over there, far off: -gari","grammar":"Locations and directions › Locations, p. 65–66","html":"<p>Another way to say ‘far’ is the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gari</span>. It is very like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span>, but it attaches to the end of a word (here, to pronouns). It often just means ‘there’, with the idea that it's far away.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-gari</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there.far</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurra-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">camp-locative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gula-gala</span><span class=\"g\">sit-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘There in his camp he sits.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 58</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-nga</span><span class=\"g\">see-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">maradhara-anha</span><span class=\"g\">possum-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungala-gari</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc-there.far</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I saw a possum up there.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 88/24</p></figure><p>In 58 the listener knows he is sitting far away, in his camp; in 88/24, the possum was up there, somewhere far away.</p>","qa":[["How is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gari</span> different from <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gari</span> attaches to the end of a word; <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span> is a word on its own."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gari</span> often mean?","‘there’, with the idea of far away."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-nga ngathu maradhara-anha nhungala-gari</span> mean?","‘I saw a possum up there.’"]]},{"id":"gampa","n":"11.5","title":"Going a long way: gampa","grammar":"Locations and directions › Locations, p. 66","html":"<p>The word <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa</span> also means ‘far away’, often about physical distance, like going a long way.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span><span class=\"g\">tomorrow</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span><span class=\"g\">1dl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa</span><span class=\"g\">far</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba-rra</span><span class=\"g\">go-away-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Tomorrow the two of us will go a long way.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 160/32</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa</span><span class=\"g\">far</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I'm going far away.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 182/54</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa</span> is often used together with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gari</span>:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-gari</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there.far</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">dog-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa</span><span class=\"g\">far</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali-ani</span><span class=\"g\">good-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That dog (far away) is good.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 78/14</p></figure><p>With <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> ‘not’, it means ‘not far’: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-gari walya gampa</span> ‘it is over there, not far away’.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘I'm going far away’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa nganyi yanhtha-gala</span>."],["How do you say ‘not far’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya gampa</span>."],["Which ending is often used together with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gari</span>."]]},{"id":"wina","n":"11.6","title":"Close by: wina","grammar":"Locations and directions › Locations, p. 66–67","html":"<p>The word for ‘close by’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina</span>. Here it is used to talk about sitting close to the fire:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina</span><span class=\"g\">close</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span><span class=\"g\">1dl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barnrdi-rra</span><span class=\"g\">cross-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina-warra</span><span class=\"g\">close-belonging</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span><span class=\"g\">1dl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">sit-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyi-lya</span><span class=\"g\">fire-there</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘We will sit down close to the fire.’</figcaption><p class=\"lit\">Literally: ‘We will move close to sit close to the fire.’</p><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 149/21</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina</span> mean?","‘close, close by’."],["Which words mean ‘far’, and which means ‘close’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galyi</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gampa</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gari</span> mean ‘far’; <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina</span> means ‘close’."],["What does 149/21 mean?","‘We will sit down close to the fire.’"]]},{"id":"dhika","n":"11.7","title":"Going away or coming back: -dhika","grammar":"Locations and directions › Directions › -dhika on verbs, p. 67–68","html":"<p>The ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhika</span> on a verb shows that <strong>going away</strong> or <strong>coming back</strong> is involved: going away from the speaker towards something else, or away from some other place towards the speaker.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-dhika-wa</span><span class=\"g\">sit-away-emphatic</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-rra</span><span class=\"g\">strike-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">1dl-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanggu-andru</span><span class=\"g\">spear-instrumental</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Don't go too close, the two of us will kill it with a spear.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 120/56</p></figure><p>The speaker tells the listener to move away from the thing they are talking about.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-dhika-rra</span><span class=\"g\">give-back-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi</span><span class=\"g\">meat</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I give back your meat to you.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 198/6</p></figure><p>The meat goes away from the speaker, back to the listener.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-dhika-rra</span><span class=\"g\">grasp-back-imperative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Bring that boomerang here.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 262/6</p></figure><p>The boomerang comes away from where it is, towards the speaker.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhika</span> add to <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya</span> ‘give’?","Giving back: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-dhika</span>."],["How do you say ‘bring’ (from <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha</span> ‘get, grasp’)?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-dhika</span>."],["What two directions can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhika</span> show?","Going away from the speaker, or coming back towards the speaker."]]},{"id":"dhikadhika","n":"11.8","title":"Going round and round: -dhika-dhika","grammar":"Locations and directions › Directions › -dhikadhika on verbs, p. 68","html":"<p>With <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhika</span> twice in a row, the meaning changes from ‘going away’ to <strong>going around in circles</strong>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidya-nga</span><span class=\"g\">father-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">dog-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-dhika-dhika-gala</span><span class=\"g\">run-back-back-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My father's dog is running around.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 95/31</p></figure><p>The dog is running round and round in circles.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nunpa-dhika-dhika</span><span class=\"g\">push-back-back</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yandra-anha</span><span class=\"g\">rock-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Roll that stone.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 405/21</p></figure><p>The stone turns round and round.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhika-dhika</span> mean?","Going around in circles."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guthi-dhika-dhika-gala</span> mean?","‘running around’."],["How do you say ‘roll (it)’, from <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nunpa</span> ‘push’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nunpa-dhika-dhika</span>."]]},{"id":"warri","n":"11.9","title":"Up and down: -warri","grammar":"Locations and directions › Directions › -warri on verbs, p. 68–69","html":"<p>The ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warri</span> on a verb shows that the movement goes <strong>up or down</strong>, above or below the speaker.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-warri</span><span class=\"g\">get-vertical</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanggu-anha</span><span class=\"g\">spear-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">/</span><span class=\"g\">/</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gatyi-li-ba-langu</span><span class=\"g\">break-apart-motion-possibility</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Pick up that spear. (It) might break.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 529/15</p></figure><p>The spear is below the speaker, on the ground, and has to be picked up.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yandra</span><span class=\"g\">rock</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buli-warri-rra</span><span class=\"g\">fall-vertical-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mirgala-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">slope-locative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-anga</span><span class=\"g\">water-dative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘This stone will fall down along the steep bank into the water.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 408/24</p></figure><p>As the stone falls, it moves further and further down until it lands in the river.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warri</span> show?","That the movement goes up or down (above or below the speaker)."],["How do you say ‘pick up’ (from <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha</span> ‘get’)?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-warri</span>."],["In 408/24, which word means ‘into the water’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-anga</span>."]]},{"id":"ba","n":"11.10","title":"Away from the speaker: -ba","grammar":"Locations and directions › Directions › -ba on verbs, p. 69","html":"<p>The ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ba</span> on a verb means the action moves <strong>away from the speaker</strong>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span><span class=\"g\">OK</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walga-ani</span><span class=\"g\">woman-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span><span class=\"g\">all</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba-rra</span><span class=\"g\">go-away-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">giga-anha</span><span class=\"g\">bullrushes-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">get-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Now all of you women go out to fetch bullrushes.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 196/4</p></figure><p>The women will go away from the speaker to look for bullrushes.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ga</span><span class=\"g\">yes</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-lha</span><span class=\"g\">sit-factive</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nangala</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">/</span><span class=\"g\">/</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba</span><span class=\"g\">go-away</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Yes, I stop here, you go on.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 459/11</p></figure><p>The speaker and the listener were walking together; now the listener goes on, away from the speaker.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ba</span> add to <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha</span> ‘go’?","Going away from the speaker: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba</span> ‘go away, go on’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini yanhtha-ba</span> mean?","‘You go on.’"],["Name the four direction endings on verbs in this unit.","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhika</span> (away or back), <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhika-dhika</span> (around), <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warri</span> (up or down) and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ba</span> (away from the speaker)."]]}]},{"n":12,"title":"Making sentences and asking questions","intro":"<p>Now that you know about verbs, nouns and their endings, you can put sentences together.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"no-verb","n":"12.1","title":"Sentences without a verb","grammar":"Putting sentences together, p. 70","html":"<p>In English, the simplest sentence has a noun and a verb (<em>I ate</em>). Sentences that say what something is use a form of <em>to be</em>: <em>I am a person</em>. Every full English sentence has a verb.</p><p>Kullilli has <strong>no verb ‘to be’</strong>, so the simplest Kullilli sentence can have no verb at all: just two words, one saying something about the other, with case endings to show how they relate.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">miti-ani</span><span class=\"g\">dirty-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mara-ani</span><span class=\"g\">hand-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Your hand is dirty.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 506/58</p></figure>","qa":[["Is there a Kullilli verb for ‘to be’?","No."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">miti-ani ingka mara-ani</span> mean?","‘Your hand is dirty.’"],["Does every Kullilli sentence need a verb?","No. A sentence can be just two words, one describing the other."]]},{"id":"word-order","n":"12.2","title":"Word order is free","grammar":"Putting sentences together, p. 70–71","html":"<p>English sentences usually go <em>subject, verb, object</em>, and that's how we know who does what: <em>The dog eats food</em> means something very different from <em>The food eats dog</em>.</p><p>Kullilli sentences don't depend on word order, because the endings show each noun's role:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-guru-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">2dl-there-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhtha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">eat-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunga-anha</span><span class=\"g\">food-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of you eat this food.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 217/25</p></figure><p>Swapping the two nouns doesn't change the meaning, because <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngu</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span> still show who is eating and what is eaten:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunga-anha</span><span class=\"g\">food-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhtha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">eat-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yula-guru-ngu</span><span class=\"g\">2dl-there-ergative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of you eat this food.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure><p>To give a word extra attention (<em>focus</em>), move it to the beginning of the sentence. There are probably some limits on word order, which are still being looked into.</p>","qa":[["Does swapping the nouns in 217/25 change the meaning?","No. The endings <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-ngu</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anha</span> still show who eats and what is eaten."],["How do you give a word extra attention?","Move it to the beginning of the sentence."],["In English, what tells you who does what in <em>The dog eats food</em>?","Word order."]]},{"id":"yes-no-questions","n":"12.3","title":"Yes-or-no questions","grammar":"Asking Questions › Polar (“yes or no”) questions, p. 72","html":"<p>English often starts a yes-or-no question with <em>Do</em> or <em>Are</em> (<em>Are you enjoying learning Kullilli?</em>). In Kullilli it's simple: put the <strong>verb at the beginning</strong> of the sentence.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganhdhuganga-gala</span><span class=\"g\">understand-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru-anha</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-there-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Do you understand them?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 245/53</p></figure><p>Also, raise the pitch of your voice at the end of the sentence, just as English speakers do in a question.</p>","qa":[["How do you make a yes-or-no question in Kullilli?","Put the verb at the beginning, and raise your voice at the end."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganhdhuganga-gala yundru dhana-guru-anha</span> mean?","‘Do you understand them?’"],["What happens to the pitch of your voice at the end of a question?","It rises."]]},{"id":"wh-questions","n":"12.4","title":"Questions with question words","grammar":"Asking Questions › Information questions, p. 72–73","html":"<p>Questions asking <em>who</em>, <em>what</em>, <em>where</em>, <em>why</em> and so on are called <em>information questions</em>. In Kullilli, put the question word (see the Question words unit) <strong>at the beginning</strong> of the sentence.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wara-lu</span><span class=\"g\">who-ergative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-nga</span><span class=\"g\">grasp-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhangala</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.loc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurra-nha</span><span class=\"g\">stick-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Who picked up my stick here?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 111/47</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span><span class=\"g\">what-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhina</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.masc.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">laugh-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Why (is he) laughing?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 327/7</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru</span><span class=\"g\">where-ablative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Where are you from?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 133/5</p></figure>","qa":[["Where does the question word go in a Kullilli question?","At the beginning."],["Which question word starts ‘Why is he laughing?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span>."],["Ask ‘Where are you from?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngala-andru ini</span>."]]},{"id":"joining","n":"12.5","title":"Joining sentences together","grammar":"Asking Questions, p. 73","html":"<p>To join related sentences in English, we use words like <em>and</em>. In Kullilli you don't need to: just say the sentences one after another and they are understood to go together.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-nga</span><span class=\"g\">grasp-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wirnbi-nga</span><span class=\"g\">throw-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyi-lunga</span><span class=\"g\">fire-locative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I picked it up and threw it in the fire.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 113/49</p></figure><p>The English translation needs <em>and</em>, but the Kullilli just puts the two actions next to each other.</p>","qa":[["Is there a word for ‘and’ in 113/49?","No. The two parts are just said one after the other."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu manhdha-nga</span> mean?","‘I picked (it) up.’"],["How do you join two related sentences in Kullilli?","Say them one after the other."]]}]},{"n":13,"title":"Reasons and feelings","intro":"<p>Saying <em>because</em> and <em>that's why</em>, and talking about how you feel.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"because","n":"13.1","title":"Because: the ablative","grammar":"Next steps › Reasons, p. 74","html":"<p>You already know how to ask ‘why?’ with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span>. To answer, Kullilli can use the <strong>ablative</strong> case. The ablative usually shows movement away from something, but it can also mean ‘because of’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-iyi-nga</span><span class=\"g\">strike-reflexive-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungurra-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.abl-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhina-anha</span><span class=\"g\">foot-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buli-nga</span><span class=\"g\">fall-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I knocked my foot because of it and fell down.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 292/36</p></figure><p>The speaker fell because they knocked their foot on something: the ablative pronoun <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungurra-guru</span> gives the reason.</p>","qa":[["Which case can mean ‘because of’?","The ablative."],["In 292/36, why did the speaker fall down?","They knocked their foot on something."],["Which word in 292/36 means ‘because of it’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungurra-guru</span>."]]},{"id":"thats-why","n":"13.2","title":"That's why: yala-anga","grammar":"Next steps › Reasons, p. 74","html":"<p>Another way to talk about reasons is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yala-anga</span> ‘that's why’. It looks like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span> ‘why?’: both have the dative ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-anga</span>. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yala</span> adds the meaning ‘that’, so <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yala-anga</span> is ‘(for) that (reason)’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yala-anga</span><span class=\"g\">that-dative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">malyu</span><span class=\"g\">anger</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-gala</span><span class=\"g\">rise-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That's why he is angry with you.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 233/41</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yala-anga</span> mean?","‘That's why.’"],["Which question word has the same ending as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yala-anga</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mina-anga</span> ‘why?’"],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yala-anga nhani-guru ingka malyu warlpa-gala</span> mean?","‘That's why he is angry with you.’"]]},{"id":"i-feel","n":"13.3","title":"How I feel: balhthi-iyi-gala","grammar":"Next steps › Feelings, p. 75","html":"<p>The sources have quite few words for feelings. That doesn't mean Kullilli people didn't have them, just that not many were written down. Here are the words and phrases we know.</p><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi-iyi-gala</span> means ‘I feel’. Word by word, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi</span> means ‘fall, come down’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> means ‘self’, and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span> means it is happening now. Combine it with words like these:</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yawura</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘hot’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhilaba</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘cold’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘good’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mipa</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘bad’</span></li></ul><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhali</span><span class=\"g\">good</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi-iyi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">feel-self-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I feel good.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yawura</span><span class=\"g\">hot</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi-iyi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">feel-self-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I feel hot.’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["Which three parts make up <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi-iyi-gala</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi</span> ‘fall, come down’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> ‘self’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span> (now)."],["How do you say ‘I feel cold’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhilaba balhthi-iyi-gala nganyi</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mipa</span> mean?","‘bad’."]]},{"id":"feel-sick","n":"13.4","title":"Feeling sick: munka-iyi-gala","grammar":"Next steps › Feelings, p. 75","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">munka</span> means ‘feel sick’. Like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi-iyi-gala</span>, it takes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> ‘self’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span> ‘now’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">munka-iyi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">feel.sick-self-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I feel sick.’</figcaption></figure><p>If you have a headache, use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">munka-iyi-gala</span> with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guka</span> ‘head’ to say your head hurts:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guka</span><span class=\"g\">head</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">munka-iyi-gala</span><span class=\"g\">feel.sick-self-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My head is hurting.’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">munka</span> mean?","‘feel sick’."],["How do you say ‘My head is hurting’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guka nganytya munka-iyi-gala</span>."],["What is the word for ‘head’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guka</span>."]]},{"id":"cry-angry","n":"13.5","title":"Crying and being angry","grammar":"Next steps › Feelings, p. 75–76","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganguri</span> is a verb meaning ‘cry’. Just add a verb ending such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span>. The first sentence is from Charlie Phillips; the second was made up by the guide's authors.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganguri-gala</span><span class=\"g\">cry-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span><span class=\"g\">that</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwani</span><span class=\"g\">child</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That child is crying.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganguri-rra</span><span class=\"g\">cry-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span><span class=\"g\">that</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warliwani</span><span class=\"g\">child</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That child will cry.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">malyu</span> means ‘anger’. It is always used with the verb <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa</span> ‘rise’, so <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">malyu warlpa-gala</span> literally means ‘anger rises’ and is used to say someone is angry.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">malyu</span><span class=\"g\">anger</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-gala</span><span class=\"g\">rise-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniyi</span><span class=\"g\">this</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garnaani</span><span class=\"g\">man</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘This man is angry.’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘That child will cry’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ganguri-rra nhaniguru warliwani</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">malyu warlpa-gala</span> mean literally, and what does it mean?","Literally ‘anger rises’; it means someone is angry."],["Which verb is always used with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">malyu</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa</span> ‘rise’."]]}]},{"n":14,"title":"Owning and belonging","intro":"<p>Kullilli has two different ways of saying <em>my</em>, <em>your</em> and <em>his/her</em>: one for things that can be separated from their owner, and one for things that can't, such as body parts.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"my-your","n":"14.1","title":"My and your: nganytya, ingka","grammar":"Next steps › More on owning, possessing, and belonging › Alienable (separable) possession, p. 76–77","html":"<p>Most things that belong to someone can be separated from them. A toy can belong to one child, but if she gives it away, it belongs to someone else. Linguists call this <em>alienable</em> (separable) possession.</p><p>For this kind of belonging, ‘my’ or ‘mine’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> (or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytyani</span>).</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span><span class=\"g\">unable</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanki-nga</span><span class=\"g\">find-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">makurrangani</span><span class=\"g\">stick</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I couldn't find my stick.’</figcaption></figure><p>The stick belongs to the speaker, even if they can't find it, but it could become someone else's stick if they gave it away.</p><p>‘Your’ is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span>. English <em>your</em> can be about one person or many, but in Kullilli these are different words: use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span> only when talking to <strong>one</strong> person.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wani</span><span class=\"g\">what</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">your</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarinya-ani</span><span class=\"g\">name</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘What's your name?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 332/12</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘my’ for something that can be separated from you?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span>."],["When do you use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span> ‘your’?","When talking to one person."],["What does <em>alienable</em> possession mean?","Owning something that can be separated from the owner (it could be given away)."]]},{"id":"his-her","n":"14.2","title":"His, her, their: nhungka","grammar":"Next steps › More on owning, possessing, and belonging › Alienable (separable) possession, p. 77–78","html":"<p>English has <em>his</em>, <em>her</em> and <em>their</em>. Kullilli has just one word, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span>, which doesn't show gender. It is translated as ‘his’ when the owner is male, ‘her’ when the owner is female, and ‘their’ when the speaker doesn't know.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span><span class=\"g\">his</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurraandru</span><span class=\"g\">camp</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidyani</span><span class=\"g\">father</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhikagala</span><span class=\"g\">go.back</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My father comes back from his camp.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span><span class=\"g\">her</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurraandru</span><span class=\"g\">camp</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walgani</span><span class=\"g\">woman</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhikagala</span><span class=\"g\">go.back</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The woman comes back from her camp.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span><span class=\"g\">their</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngurraandru</span><span class=\"g\">camp</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bariani</span><span class=\"g\">stranger</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhikagala</span><span class=\"g\">go.back</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The stranger comes back from their camp.’</figcaption></figure><p>Some common things to talk about:</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya ngarnidya</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘my father’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka ngarnidya</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘your father’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka dhithi</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘your dog’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka yarinya</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘your name’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka yarinya</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘his name’</span></li></ul>","qa":[["How many Kullilli words are there for ‘his’, ‘her’ and ‘their’?","One: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span>."],["How do you say ‘your dog’ (to one person)?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka dhithi</span>."],["How would you translate <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span> when the owner is a woman?","‘her’."]]},{"id":"body-parts","n":"14.3","title":"My hand: body parts","grammar":"Next steps › More on owning, possessing, and belonging › Inalienable (inseparable) possession, p. 78","html":"<p>Some things can't exist apart from their owner, like a person's spirit. This is <em>inalienable</em> (inseparable) possession. In Kullilli, <strong>body parts</strong> belong to this group, and a different word for ‘my’ is used. Compare:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithi</span><span class=\"g\">dog</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mipa</span><span class=\"g\">bad</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My dog is bad.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mara-ani</span><span class=\"g\">hand</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhuka-gala</span><span class=\"g\">swell-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My hand is swelling.’</figcaption></figure><p>Both <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> mean ‘my’, but <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> is used for things that can be separated from the owner, and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> for things, like body parts, that can't. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> usually means ‘I’, but next to a noun it can mean ‘my’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">iliri</span><span class=\"g\">tears</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhti-gala</span><span class=\"g\">fall-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My tears are running.’</figcaption></figure><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi dhina</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘my foot’</span></li></ul><p>There are a few examples of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> with body parts too, such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla guka nganytya murta-rra</span> ‘Tomorrow my head will hurt’. We have very few examples like this, so it's hard to say why. Overall, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> is the usual way to say ‘my’ with a body part.</p>","qa":[["Which ‘my’ is usually used with body parts?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span>."],["How do you say ‘my foot’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi dhina</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> usually mean, apart from ‘my’?","‘I’."]]},{"id":"body-parts-2","n":"14.4","title":"Your hand, his leg","grammar":"Next steps › More on owning, possessing, and belonging › Inalienable (inseparable) possession, p. 79","html":"<p>For ‘your (body part)’, use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span> instead of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span>. Like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span> has two meanings: ‘you’ and ‘your’. This sentence is from Charlie Phillips:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">minha</span><span class=\"g\">what</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">munkaiyi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">happen-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span><span class=\"g\">your</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mara-ani</span><span class=\"g\">hand</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘What happened to your hand?’</figcaption></figure><p>For ‘his, her, its (body part)’, use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span> instead of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span>; it also means ‘he, she, it’.</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru nantyinyanha</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘his shin’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">watyana nhaniguru</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘his leg’</span></li></ul><p>So for body parts, the words for ‘my, your, his/her’ are the nominative pronouns. The important thing to remember is that there are two ways to talk about things that belong to people:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Can be separated from the owner</th><th>Can't be separated (mostly body parts)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>my</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span></td></tr><tr><td>your</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini</span></td></tr><tr><td>his, her</td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span></td><td><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>","qa":[["How do you say ‘your hand’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ini mara-ani</span>."],["Which word means ‘his’ with a body part?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhaniguru</span>."],["Which ‘my’ goes with ‘dog’, and which with ‘hand’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> with ‘dog’, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> with ‘hand’."]]},{"id":"warra-mine","n":"14.5","title":"Mine, yours: -warra","grammar":"Next steps › More on owning, possessing, and belonging › Belonging to: The suffix -warra, p. 79–80","html":"<p>The ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warra</span> means ‘belonging to’. On a possessive pronoun like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span> ‘my’, it makes a word meaning ‘mine’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya-warra</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.dat-belonging</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngalka-nga</span><span class=\"g\">think-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anha</span><span class=\"g\">meat-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I thought this meat here was mine.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 166/38</p></figure><p>There are no recorded sentences with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warra</span> on the other possessive pronouns, but the same pattern gives these forms (made up by the guide's authors):</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya-warra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘mine’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka-warra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘yours’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka-warra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘his, hers’</span></li></ul><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka-warra</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat-belonging</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngalka-nga</span><span class=\"g\">think-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-anha</span><span class=\"g\">meat-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I thought this meat here was yours.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘mine’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya-warra</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warra</span> mean?","‘belonging to’."],["Is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka-warra</span> ‘yours’ found in the recorded sentences?","No. The authors made it up following the pattern of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya-warra</span>."]]},{"id":"warra-questions","n":"14.6","title":"How many? When? -warra again","grammar":"Next steps › More on owning, possessing, and belonging › Belonging to: The suffix -warra, p. 80–81","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warra</span> has other uses too. It is on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina</span> ‘close’ in <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina-warra</span> ‘close (to)’ (see the lesson on <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wina</span>). And it is used to make questions about <strong>quantity</strong> and <strong>time</strong>.</p><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdhu</span> both mean ‘much’, but <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> is about amounts of things and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdhu</span> is about time. With <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-warra</span>:</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru-warra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘how much? how many?’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdhu-warra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘when?’</span></li></ul><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdha-warra</span><span class=\"g\">much-belonging</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhdhra-ani</span><span class=\"g\">kangaroo-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘How many kangaroos are there?’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 513/65</p></figure><aside class=\"note\"><p>In this recorded sentence the word is written <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdha-warra</span>, which looks more like the ‘time’ word, so this still needs checking.</p></aside>","qa":[["How would you ask ‘when?’","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdhu-warra</span>."],["What is the difference between <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdhu</span>?","Both mean ‘much’; <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru</span> is about amounts of things, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanhdhu</span> about time."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wandru-warra</span> mean?","‘how much? how many?’"]]}]},{"n":15,"title":"Talking about time","intro":"<p>Words like <em>yesterday</em>, <em>tomorrow</em> and <em>soon</em>. These words are relative to the day you say them, not exact days or times like <em>1 January</em> or <em>2 pm</em>.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"yesterday","n":"15.1","title":"Yesterday: niladha","grammar":"More next steps › Relative time, p. 82","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> means ‘yesterday’. Put it at the beginning or end of a sentence about something that happened the day before. These sentences were said by Charlie Phillips, who spoke Kullilli as his first language.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gali</span><span class=\"g\">ask-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">I.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhinha</span><span class=\"g\">him</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span><span class=\"g\">yesterday</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I asked him yesterday.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span><span class=\"g\">yesterday</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gidani</span><span class=\"g\">cattle</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span><span class=\"g\">all</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wagabala-gali</span><span class=\"g\">lay-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhangala</span><span class=\"g\">at.this.place</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Yesterday all the cattle camped here.’</figcaption></figure><p>Remember that the past ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span> seems to be used when there's a time word like <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> in the sentence.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> mean?","‘yesterday’."],["Where in the sentence can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> go?","At the beginning or the end."],["How do you say ‘I asked him yesterday’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-gali ngathu nhinha niladha</span>."]]},{"id":"today-tomorrow","n":"15.2","title":"Today and tomorrow: bala, wikarla","grammar":"More next steps › Relative time, p. 82–83","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bala</span> means ‘today’. At the end of a sentence it says the event happened, or will happen, on the same day.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhulu</span><span class=\"g\">he</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhtha-gali</span><span class=\"g\">eat-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gungaanha</span><span class=\"g\">food</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bala</span><span class=\"g\">today</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘He didn't eat any food today.’</figcaption></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span> means ‘tomorrow’. Put it at the beginning or end of a sentence about something that will happen the next day.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngarnidya</span><span class=\"g\">father</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika-gala</span><span class=\"g\">go.back-is</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span><span class=\"g\">tomorrow</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘My father is coming back tomorrow.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span><span class=\"g\">tomorrow</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">guka</span><span class=\"g\">head</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">my</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">murta-rra</span><span class=\"g\">hurt-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Tomorrow my head will hurt.’</figcaption></figure><p>One way to practise these words is to use them in English sentences with family and friends: instead of <em>we need to buy milk tomorrow</em>, say <em>we need to buy milk <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span></em>.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bala</span> mean?","‘today’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span> mean?","‘tomorrow’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya nhulu dhalhtha-gali gungaanha bala</span> mean?","‘He didn't eat any food today.’"]]},{"id":"two-days","n":"15.3","title":"Two days away: -watyu","grammar":"More next steps › Relative time, p. 83","html":"<p>Kullilli doesn't always need as many words as English. <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla-watyu</span> is one word, but in English it takes four: ‘the day after tomorrow’. It is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla</span> ‘tomorrow’ with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> ‘one’: tomorrow plus one.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla-watyu</span><span class=\"g\">tomorrow-one</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika-ra</span><span class=\"g\">go.back-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I will return the day after tomorrow.’</figcaption></figure><p>In the same way, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> ‘yesterday’ with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> makes <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha-watyu</span> ‘the day before yesterday’. (This example was made up by the guide's authors.)</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha-watyu</span><span class=\"g\">yesterday-one</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhulu</span><span class=\"g\">he</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhtha-gali</span><span class=\"g\">eat-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gungaanha</span><span class=\"g\">food</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘He didn't eat any food the day before yesterday.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Made up by the guide’s authors</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘the day after tomorrow’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla-watyu</span>."],["How is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha-watyu</span> made, and what does it mean?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span> ‘yesterday’ plus <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-watyu</span> ‘one’; it means ‘the day before yesterday’."],["How many English words does it take to say <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wikarla-watyu</span>?","Four: ‘the day after tomorrow’."]]},{"id":"soon","n":"15.4","title":"Soon: gurnrtu","grammar":"More next steps › Relative time, p. 83; Temporal particles, p. 85","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu</span> means ‘soon’: something that will happen in the near future. We don't know exactly how far in the future it can be used for, but it is a useful word.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu-tyiri</span><span class=\"g\">soon-?</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mirla-ani</span><span class=\"g\">sun-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">warlpa-ba-rra</span><span class=\"g\">rise-emphatic-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Soon the sun will come up.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 318/62</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu</span> mean?","‘soon’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu-tyiri mirla-ani warlpa-ba-rra</span> mean?","‘Soon the sun will come up.’"],["Do we know exactly how far in the future <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu</span> can refer to?","No, not yet."]]},{"id":"late-long-ago","n":"15.5","title":"Late and long ago: maltya, matya","grammar":"More next steps › Other ways to talk about time: Temporal particles, p. 84","html":"<p>Some other words mark points in time. They are sometimes called <em>temporal particles</em>.</p><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">maltya</span> means ‘late’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mirla-ani</span><span class=\"g\">sun-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">maltya</span><span class=\"g\">late</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galguri-gala</span><span class=\"g\">set-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The sun is going down – late.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 156/28</p></figure><p>The present ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span> shows the sun is still going down, but later than expected.</p><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">matya</span>, which sounds similar, means ‘long ago’:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">matya</span><span class=\"g\">long.ago</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhika-manytya-nga-ba</span><span class=\"g\">go.back-move-past-emphatic</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garna-bula</span><span class=\"g\">man-dual</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Long ago two people went past.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 391/7</p></figure><p>Here the past ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span> shows the event was over, and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">matya</span> shows it happened a long time ago.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">maltya</span> mean?","‘late’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">matya</span> mean?","‘long ago’."],["Why is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span> used in 391/7?","Because the event (two people going past) was completely over."]]},{"id":"not-yet-while","n":"15.6","title":"Not yet, and a while: gadha, dhuna","grammar":"More next steps › Other ways to talk about time: Temporal particles, p. 84–85","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gadha</span> means ‘yet’. It is used with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> ‘not’ to mean ‘not yet’.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gadha</span><span class=\"g\">yet</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mulpa-gala</span><span class=\"g\">finish-present</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I haven't finished (it) yet.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 362/42</p></figure><p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhuna</span> means something like ‘a while’, usually about waiting for something:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhuna-wira</span><span class=\"g\">time-wait</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu-nu</span><span class=\"g\">soon-?</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bunhthi-na-rra</span><span class=\"g\">come-?-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Wait a while, soon they will all come past.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 390/6</p></figure><p>This sentence also has <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gurnrtu</span> ‘soon’.</p>","qa":[["How do you say ‘not yet’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya gadha</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhuna</span> mean?","‘a while’ (usually about waiting)."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya gadha ngathu mulpa-gala</span> mean?","‘I haven't finished (it) yet.’"]]}]},{"n":16,"title":"Commands, each other, and ‘I think’","intro":"<p>Telling people what to do, doing things to yourself or to each other, and showing you're not sure.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"commands-bare","n":"16.1","title":"Commands: just the verb","grammar":"More next steps › Giving instructions, p. 85–86","html":"<p>It's useful to be able to give instructions. Kullilli has two ways to say commands. The first is to use the verb with <strong>no ending</strong>: none of <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gala</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-gali</span>, <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nga</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span>. A bare verb is understood as a command, much like English <em>go</em>, <em>stop</em>, <em>come</em>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawa</span><span class=\"g\">come</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganala</span><span class=\"g\">to.me</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Come to me.’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya</span><span class=\"g\">give</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">me.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Give me (it).’</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">garki</span><span class=\"g\">call</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">your</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhithianha</span><span class=\"g\">dog</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Call your dog off.’</figcaption></figure><p>These commands don't include ‘you’, but you can include it:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhanha</span><span class=\"g\">that</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthianha</span><span class=\"g\">meat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhalhtha</span><span class=\"g\">eat</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Eat that meat.’</figcaption></figure>","qa":[["What is the first way to make a command?","Use the verb with no ending."],["How do you say ‘Come to me’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gawa nganala</span>."],["Do you have to say ‘you’ in a command?","No, but you can."]]},{"id":"commands-rra","n":"16.2","title":"Commands with -rra","grammar":"More next steps › Giving instructions, p. 86","html":"<p>The second way is to put <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> on the verb. Here are some common commands to practise every day:</p><ul class=\"words\"><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘go’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gari-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘stop’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘sit’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhina-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘stand’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaltha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘eat’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngutya-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘give’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘speak’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gunka-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘ask’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘get (something)’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-dhika-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘bring (something)’</span></li><li><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walka-rra</span> <span class=\"gl\">‘put (something somewhere)’</span></li></ul><p>There are two different verbs written <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha</span>: one means ‘go’ and the other means ‘speak’.</p><p>This ending looks just like the future <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span>, and sometimes it may mean both. Think of a parent telling a child <em>You will stay home</em>: it's future, but it's also a command. These sentences could be read either way:</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gapa-rra</span><span class=\"g\">follow-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganha</span><span class=\"g\">me.acc</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You will follow me.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 380/60</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyianha</span><span class=\"g\">firewood</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">you</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhati-rra</span><span class=\"g\">chop-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘You will chop firewood.’</figcaption></figure><p>The guide's authors noticed that the bare command seems to be used more when talking to one person. Using <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> might also be more polite. We're not sure yet.</p>","qa":[["What is the second way to make a command?","Add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> to the verb."],["How do you say ‘sit!’ with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gulha-rra</span>."],["Why can <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru gapa-rra nganha</span> be a statement or a command?","Because the command ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span> looks the same as the future ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-rra</span>."]]},{"id":"reflexive","n":"16.3","title":"Doing it to yourself: -iyi","grammar":"More next steps › Doing things to each other and oneself › -iyi reflexive, p. 87","html":"<p>When someone does something to themself, add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> to the verb. This is called a <em>reflexive</em> action. The subject pronoun is the nominative (such as <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span>), and the ‘myself’ or ‘himself’ is understood from <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span>.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-iyi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">strike-reflexive-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I hit myself.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 87/23</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-iyi-gali</span><span class=\"g\">strike-reflexive-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">he.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">daranya-andru</span><span class=\"g\">axe-instrumental</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘He hit himself with a stone axe.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 86/22</p></figure><p>You have also seen <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> in the feeling words <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">balhthi-iyi-gala</span> ‘I feel’ and <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">munka-iyi-gala</span> ‘I feel sick’.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> show?","That someone does something to themself (reflexive)."],["How do you say ‘I hit myself’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">galka-iyi-gali nganyi</span>."],["Which ‘I’ is used with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span>: <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span> or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span>."]]},{"id":"reciprocal","n":"16.4","title":"Doing it to each other: -nyara, -nyala","grammar":"More next steps › Doing things to each other and oneself › -nyala/nyara: reciprocal, p. 87–88","html":"<p>When several people do the same thing to each other, so that each one both does it and has it done to them, add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nyara</span> (or <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nyala</span>) to the verb. This is called a <em>reciprocal</em> action.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span><span class=\"g\">1dl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-nyara-rra</span><span class=\"g\">see-reciprocal-future</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘The two of us will look at each other.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 510/62</p></figure><p>Each of the two looks, and is looked at.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yamba-nyara-gala</span><span class=\"g\">fight-reciprocal-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walga-ni</span><span class=\"g\">woman-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">buka</span><span class=\"g\">all</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhana-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3pl-there</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘That group of women are having a fight.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 343/23</p></figure><p>Each woman fights, and is fought by another in the group.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span><span class=\"g\">OK</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali</span><span class=\"g\">1dl.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-nyala-gali</span><span class=\"g\">see-reciprocal-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">niladha</span><span class=\"g\">yesterday</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘OK, the two of us were looking at each other yesterday.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 293/37</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nyara</span> show?","That people do something to each other (reciprocal)."],["How is <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nyara</span> different from <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-iyi</span> is doing something to yourself; <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-nyara</span> is doing it to each other."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngali nadya-nyara-rra</span> mean?","‘The two of us will look at each other.’"]]},{"id":"bari","n":"16.5","title":"I think…: bari","grammar":"More next steps › Showing Uncertainty: The verb bari ‘to think’, p. 88–89","html":"<p>In English you can show you're not sure by saying <em>I think…</em>, instead of <em>I know…</em> or just stating it. Kullilli does the same with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span> ‘think’: add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span> to any sentence to show you aren't certain it's true.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wanki-rra</span><span class=\"g\">find-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span><span class=\"g\">think</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngandra</span><span class=\"g\">we.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi-anga</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang-dative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I think we will look for (and find) your boomerang.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 341/21</p></figure><p>The speaker isn't sure they will find the boomerang.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">go-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span><span class=\"g\">think</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">I.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">gubi-anga</span><span class=\"g\">doctor-dative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I will go to the doctor, I think.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 439/55</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span><span class=\"g\">think</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-ba-nga</span><span class=\"g\">go-away-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yarlaguba</span><span class=\"g\">somewhere</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘It hasn't gone away, I think.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 372/52</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you show you aren't sure about something?","Add <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bari</span> ‘think’ to the sentence."],["How would you say ‘You will go to the doctor, I think’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhtha-rra bari ini gubi-anga</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya bari yanhtha-ba-nga yarlaguba</span> mean?","‘It hasn't gone away, I think.’"]]}]},{"n":17,"title":"Saying no","intro":"<p>Saying ‘not’ and ‘no’, ‘without’, and ‘unable’.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"not-walya","n":"17.1","title":"Not: walya","grammar":"Walya ‘not, no’ › Examples of ‘not’, p. 90","html":"<p><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> is the word for ‘not’ and ‘no’. It usually comes at the beginning of the sentence.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">murta-gala</span><span class=\"g\">hurt-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I am not sick.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 46</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">girni-ani</span><span class=\"g\">heavy-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘This is not heavy.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 170/42</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhani-guru</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.nom-there</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ingka</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">bakaranyi-ani</span><span class=\"g\">boomerang-nominative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘This is not your boomerang.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 102/38</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">inha</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.acc</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-gala</span><span class=\"g\">see-present</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">/</span><span class=\"g\">/</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yuru</span><span class=\"g\">only</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walbiri-anha</span><span class=\"g\">shade-accusative</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I don't see you – only your shadow.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 91/27</p></figure>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> mean?","‘not’ or ‘no’."],["How do you say ‘I am not sick’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya murta-gala nganyi</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya girni-ani nhani</span> mean?","‘This is not heavy.’"]]},{"id":"without","n":"17.2","title":"Without: -munhdhu","grammar":"Walya ‘not, no’ › Privative suffix, p. 91","html":"<p>The ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-munhdhu</span> on a noun means ‘without’: that thing is missing from the situation.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-munhdhu</span><span class=\"g\">meat-without</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I am without meat.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 75/11</p></figure><p>The speaker has no meat at the moment.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mura</span><span class=\"g\">finished</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">mara-ni</span><span class=\"g\">hand-nominative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganytya</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.dat</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaka-munhdhu</span><span class=\"g\">dirt-without</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Already my hand is clean.’</figcaption><p class=\"lit\">Literally: ‘My hand is without dirt.’</p><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 508/60 · Part of a longer sentence</p></figure><p>The speaker has washed their hand, so it is ‘without dirt’.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-munhdhu</span> mean?","‘without’."],["How do you say ‘I am without meat’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nguthi-munhdhu nganyi</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">dhaka-munhdhu</span> mean?","‘without dirt’ (clean)."]]},{"id":"there-is-no","n":"17.3","title":"There's no…: walya and -munhdhu","grammar":"Walya ‘not, no’ › Walyu in the sense of ‘no’, p. 90","html":"<p>To say there is none of something (<em>There's no water</em>), use <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> together with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-munhdhu</span> ‘without’ on the noun. We aren't sure yet whether <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> is needed every time.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngaka-munhdhu</span><span class=\"g\">water-without</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘No water here.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 26</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yanhdha</span><span class=\"g\">go</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyi-anha</span><span class=\"g\">firewood-accusative</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">manhdha-rra</span><span class=\"g\">get-future</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">yundru</span><span class=\"g\">2sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">/</span><span class=\"g\">/</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span><span class=\"g\">not</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wiyi-munhdhu</span><span class=\"g\">fire-without</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘Go get wood, there's no fire.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 15</p></figure>","qa":[["How do you say ‘No water here’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya ngaka-munhdhu</span>."],["Which two words or endings are used to say ‘there's no…’?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> ‘not’ and the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-munhdhu</span> ‘without’."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya wiyi-munhdhu</span> mean?","‘There's no fire.’"]]},{"id":"unable","n":"17.4","title":"Unable: barninyi","grammar":"Walya ‘not, no’, p. 91","html":"<p>Another way to say ‘no’ is to say that something <strong>can't</strong> happen. The word <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span> means ‘unable’. It often comes with the ending <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhali</span> on the verb, which seems to mean that someone is unable to do something in this particular situation.</p><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span><span class=\"g\">unable</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nganyi</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.nom</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">wira-dhali-nga</span><span class=\"g\">wait-ability-past</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nhungka</span><span class=\"g\">3sg.dat</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I can't wait for it.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 298/42</p></figure><figure class=\"ex\"><div class=\"igt\"><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span><span class=\"g\">unable</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">ngathu</span><span class=\"g\">1sg.erg</span></span><span class=\"w\"><span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">nadya-dhali-nga</span><span class=\"g\">see-ability-past</span></span></div><figcaption class=\"tr\">‘I could not see it.’</figcaption><p class=\"src\">Recorded sentence 300/44</p></figure><p>You have seen <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span> before, in ‘I couldn't find his boomerang’. We don't know yet exactly how saying ‘can't’ with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span> differs from using <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">walya</span> ‘not’ with a verb.</p>","qa":[["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span> mean?","‘unable’."],["Which ending often goes on the verb with <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi</span>?","<span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">-dhali</span>."],["What does <span class=\"k\" lang=\"gll\" translate=\"no\">barninyi ngathu nadya-dhali-nga</span> mean?","‘I could not see it.’"]]}]}]};
